#
# prima-materia — chaos magic: Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut
#
# The harvest of one work of the corpus of pmt:ChaosMagic, kept apart from
# traditions/chaos-magic.ttl, which declares the tradition and the work
# (pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut) and carries its reading notes and harvest counts.
# Harvested complete under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction
# passes per part and a reading of the whole text: first Liber Null, then
# Psychonaut, each in the order of the work. Edition: York Beach, Samuel
# Weiser, 1987, fifth printing of 1997, cited by page from the running heads.
#

#
# Liber Null (Carroll), harvested complete under prima-materia#893, in the order
# of the work: front matter, Liber MMM, the initiate syllabuses Liber LUX and
# Liber NOX, the Millenium, Liber AOM. Within each part the nodes follow the text.
#

pmc:ChaosMagicHealthCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The rituals and exercises are for readers in good health only"@en, "Die Rituale und Übungen nur für Leser bei guter Gesundheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's note says the book contains powerful rituals and that its rituals and exercises should be performed only by readers in good health; anyone with heart disease, epilepsy or any chronic disease is asked not to use the material at all, and author and publisher decline responsibility for misuse and for whatever may occur."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning is directed at the whole of the material, so it points at the work rather than at one practice. The note gives no reason beyond the power of the rituals; the strain named elsewhere, near-unconsciousness in the death posture, pain, fasting, sleeplessness, drugs, is what it covers."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Author's Note, front matter (unpaginated); read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAlteredStatesOfConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Altered states of consciousness as the key to magical ability, developed without any symbolic system but reality"@en, "Veränderte Bewusstseinszustände als Schlüssel zur magischen Fähigkeit, entwickelt ohne ein anderes Symbolsystem als die Wirklichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two themes the introduction names for the whole book: altered states of consciousness unlock one's magical abilities, and these abilities can be developed with no symbolic system except reality itself. Magic in the order is called intensely practical, personal and experimental, and half the book is said to be given to the black arts, since an inclination toward the darker side of magic is as good a starting point as any."@en ;
    skos:note "The introduction names the order's ancestry as the Zos Kia Cultus and the A.'.A.'.; Diagram 1 traces a survival of the magical tradition through Egyptian, Templar, Hermetic, alchemical, Rosicrucian, Goetic, Masonic, Illuminati, Golden Dawn and Ordo Templi Orientis lines to Spare. That is the work's own account of its descent, a reception statement, and it is kept here and not as a claim of transmission."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Introduction, p. 7; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIOTGrades a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The order without formal hierarchy: students, initiates, adepts and masters"@en, "Der Orden ohne formale Hierarchie: Schüler, Initiaten, Adepten und Meister"@de ;
    skos:definition "The secrets of magic are called universal and practical, and those who realise and practise them are said to have achieved mastership; masters inspire adepts to found orders that bring others to mastership, and the mysteries are said to be preservable only by constant revelation. The order takes its name from Eros and Thanatos, sex and death, the positive and negative methods of reaching magical consciousness, illumination being the inspiration, enlightenment and liberation that success with them brings. Its stated purpose is to help decide the form of the embryonic fifth aeon by spreading magical knowledge to individuals. It has no formal hierarchy, only a division of activity by ability: students strengthen their will against their own minds and explore self-change in dream and magic; initiates acquaint themselves with all forms of attainment, perfect one, and help aspirants; adepts seek perfection in all aspects of magical power, wisdom and liberation; masters pursue the aims of the order as they see fit. The book itself is laid out by the same steps, Liber MMM for the student in the fourth degree, Liber LUX and Liber NOX for the initiate in the third, Liber AOM for the adept in the second."@en ;
    skos:note "The mastership reached by realising and practising the secrets of magic is stated as attainable but given no procedure here; the procedures stand in the syllabuses, and the last step to the degree of master is integral reincarnation (pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Order and the Quest, pp. 9-10; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, title page of Liber Null and Contents, front matter (unpaginated); read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicErosNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Eros, god of sex, one of the two gods the order is named from"@en, "Eros, Gott der Sexualität, einer der beiden Götter, nach denen der Orden benannt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Eros is named as the god of sex, from whom with Thanatos the order takes its name; sex is said to stand for the positive method of attaining magical consciousness."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Eros" ;
    pm:nameRole "god" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Order and the Quest, p. 9; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThanatosNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Thanatos, god of death, the other god of the order's name"@en, "Thanatos, Gott des Todes, der andere Gott im Namen des Ordens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Thanatos is named as the god of death, from whom with Eros the order takes its name; death is said to stand for the negative method of attaining magical consciousness, and the Rites of Thanateros of Liber NOX carry the name."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Thanatos" ;
    pm:nameRole "god" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Order and the Quest, p. 9; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 68; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical trance: concentration built up in stages until the mind enters a trance-like condition"@en, "Magische Trance: stufenweise aufgebaute Konzentration, bis der Geist in einen tranceartigen Zustand eintritt"@de ;
    skos:definition "To work magic effectively the ability to concentrate attention must be built up until the mind can enter a trance-like condition. The stages are fixed: absolute motionlessness of the body, regulation of the breathing, stopping of thoughts, then concentration on sound, on objects and on mental images. The abilities are called highly abnormal and usually inaccessible to human consciousness, demanding inhuman concentration, with great rewards. Liber MMM, of which this is the first part, also covers personal metamorphosis and the basic techniques of magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated: the three methods of attaining magical trance will yield results only if pursued with the most fanatical and morbid determination. The determination is not measured, so any absence of result can be assigned to its lack."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Image Concentration, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, introduction, p. 13; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMotionlessness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Motionlessness: holding a comfortable posture without blinking or moving, five minutes at least and fifteen before going on"@en, "Bewegungslosigkeit: eine bequeme Haltung ohne Blinzeln oder Bewegung halten, mindestens fünf Minuten und fünfzehn, bevor es weitergeht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The body is arranged in any comfortable position and held as long as possible, without blinking or moving tongue, fingers or any part; the mind is not let run on trains of thought but observes itself passively. A position that seemed comfortable may become agonising, and the student is to persist. Time is set aside daily, and every chance of inactivity used; results go into the diary. Less than five minutes is not to satisfy; when fifteen have been achieved the student proceeds to breathing."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: less than five minutes is named as an unsatisfactory result. Sign of success: fifteen minutes of stillness completes the stage. Both are durations the student can time, which makes this the most checkable stage of the syllabus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Motionlessness, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBreathing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Breathing: slower and deeper with the whole capacity of the lungs, the attention wholly on the cycle for thirty minutes"@en, "Atmung: langsamer und tiefer mit dem ganzen Lungenraum, die Aufmerksamkeit dreißig Minuten ganz beim Zyklus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Staying as motionless as possible the student deliberately makes the breathing slower and deeper, using the whole capacity of the lungs without undue effort or strain; the lungs may be held empty or full between breaths to lengthen the cycle. The important thing is that the mind directs its complete attention to the breath cycle. When this can be done for thirty minutes the student proceeds to not-thinking."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: thirty minutes of complete attention on the breath completes the stage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Breathing, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHealthFromStillness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Possibly improved health from motionlessness and breathing"@en, "Möglicherweise bessere Gesundheit aus Bewegungslosigkeit und Atmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exercises of motionlessness and breathing are said possibly to improve health, while having no other intrinsic value than as a preparation for not-thinking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Not-Thinking, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMotionlessnessYieldsHealth a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Motionlessness and breathing are said possibly to improve health"@en, "Bewegungslosigkeit und Atmung sollen möglicherweise die Gesundheit bessern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work allows that the first two exercises may improve health, and denies them any other value of their own."@en ;
    skos:note "The breathing exercise makes the same claim in the same sentence; one yielding is entered for the pair."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMotionlessness ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicHealthFromStillness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Not-Thinking, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNotThinking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Not-thinking: withdrawing the mind from every arising thought and lengthening the periods of quiescence"@en, "Nicht-Denken: den Geist von jedem aufsteigenden Gedanken abziehen und die Zeiten der Stille verlängern"@de ;
    skos:definition "While motionless and breathing deeply, the student withdraws the mind from any thought that arises. The attempt is said inevitably to reveal the mind as a raging tempest, so that only the greatest determination wins a few seconds of silence, which already counts as a triumph. The aim is complete vigilance over the arising of thoughts and ever longer periods of total quiescence; the exercise is practised at set times and whenever inactivity offers, and recorded in the diary."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success, as the work states it: even a few seconds of mental silence is called a triumph."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Not-Thinking, pp. 14-15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNotThinkingYieldsMagicalTrance a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Not-thinking is the beginning of the magical trance"@en, "Das Nicht-Denken ist der Anfang der magischen Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "Not-thinking is called the beginnings of the magical trance condition, for which motionlessness and breathing are only preparation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicNotThinking ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Not-Thinking, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMotionlessnessBeforeBreathing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Fifteen minutes of motionlessness before breathing, thirty minutes of breathing before not-thinking"@en, "Fünfzehn Minuten Bewegungslosigkeit vor der Atmung, dreißig Minuten Atmung vor dem Nicht-Denken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The stages are to be taken in order, each opened by a stated duration in the one before: fifteen minutes of motionlessness before the regulation of breathing, thirty minutes of attentive breathing before not-thinking."@en ;
    skos:note "The second step of the chain, breathing before not-thinking, is carried in the same node because the work states both thresholds in the same way: as the point at which to proceed, with no consequence of proceeding early."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicBreathing ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMotionlessness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Motionlessness, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Breathing, p. 14; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLustOfResult a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic as change in conformity with will, and lust of result as what short-circuits it"@en, "Magie als Veränderung in Übereinstimmung mit dem Willen, und die Gier nach Ergebnis als das, was sie kurzschließt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic is defined as the science and art of causing change to occur in conformity with will, and the will is said to become magically effective only when the mind is focused and not interfering with it. The mind must first discipline itself to focus its whole attention on some meaningless phenomenon: if concentration is aimed at a desire, the effect is short-circuited by lust of result, and egotistical identification, fear of failure and the reciprocal desire not to achieve desire, arising from our dual nature, destroy the result. Subjects for concentration are therefore chosen without spiritual, egotistical, intellectual, emotional or useful significance."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with its mechanism: concentration aimed at an object of desire fails through lust of result. It is the work's explanation for failure throughout; the sigil (pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction) is its answer to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, The Magical Trances, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicObjectConcentration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Object concentration: a fixed, unwavering dead stare at a meaningless object, held for hours"@en, "Objektkonzentration: ein starrer, unbeweglicher toter Blick auf einen bedeutungslosen Gegenstand, stundenlang gehalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any object will do, a mark on a wall, something in the distance, a star. To hold it with an absolutely fixed gaze for more than a few moments is called extraordinarily difficult, yet it must be persisted in for hours at a time, every attempt of the eye to distort the object and every attempt of the mind to think of something else being resisted. The ability must be developed on meaningless objects."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFixedDeadStare a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The fixed dead stare of wizards, from which the legend of the evil eye derives"@en, "Der starre tote Blick der Zauberer, aus dem die Legende vom bösen Blick stammt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The legend of the evil eye is said to derive from the ability of wizards and sorcerers to give a fixed dead stare, an ability that can be practised against any object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExtractingOccultSecrets a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Extracting occult secrets from things by the fixed gaze"@en, "Okkulte Geheimnisse aus den Dingen durch den starren Blick herausziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Eventually, the work says, it becomes possible to extract occult secrets from things by the technique of the fixed gaze."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicObjectConcentrationYieldsDeadStare a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Object concentration is said to develop the fixed dead stare"@en, "Die Objektkonzentration soll den starren toten Blick ausbilden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to give a fixed dead stare is practised against any object, and object concentration is its exercise."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicObjectConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFixedDeadStare ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicObjectConcentrationYieldsOccultSecrets a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Object concentration is said eventually to extract occult secrets from things"@en, "Die Objektkonzentration soll schließlich okkulte Geheimnisse aus den Dingen ziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Eventually it is possible to extract occult secrets from things by this technique."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicObjectConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicExtractingOccultSecrets ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMeaninglessObjectsFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The fixed gaze is developed on meaningless objects before it is turned to secrets"@en, "Der starre Blick wird an bedeutungslosen Gegenständen entwickelt, bevor er sich Geheimnissen zuwendet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to extract occult secrets from things must be developed by working with meaningless objects."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicObjectConcentrationYieldsOccultSecrets ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicObjectConcentration ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Object Concentration, p. 15; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSoundConcentration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sound concentration: one chosen sound repeated in the mind to block all other thought"@en, "Klangkonzentration: ein gewählter Laut im Geist wiederholt, um alle anderen Gedanken zu sperren"@de ;
    skos:definition "A simple sound of one or more syllables is chosen, the book's examples being Aum or Om, Abrahadabra, Yod He Vau He, Aum Mani Padme Hum, Zazas Zazas and Nasatanada Zazas, and repeated over and over in the mind to block all other thoughts; however inappropriate the choice seems, the student must persist with it."@en ;
    skos:note "Signs of success, called encouraging: the sound comes to seem to repeat itself automatically, and may occur in sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Sound Concentration, pp. 15-16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVerbalMindControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical control of the part of the mind where verbal thought arises, the key to words of power"@en, "Magische Herrschaft über den Teil des Geistes, in dem sprachliches Denken entsteht, der Schlüssel zu Machtworten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The part of the mind in which verbal thoughts arise is said to be brought under magical control by concentration on imagined sounds, and this control is called the key to words of power and certain forms of spell casting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Sound Concentration, pp. 15-16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSoundConcentrationYieldsVerbalControl a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sound concentration is said to bring verbal thought under magical control"@en, "Die Klangkonzentration soll das sprachliche Denken unter magische Herrschaft bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Concentration on mentally imagined sounds brings the verbal part of the mind under magical control and is the key to words of power and some spell casting."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSoundConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicVerbalMindControl ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Sound Concentration, pp. 15-16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicImageConcentration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Image concentration: a simple shape held undistorted in the mind's eye, at first with closed eyes"@en, "Bildkonzentration: eine einfache Form unverzerrt vor dem inneren Auge gehalten, zunächst mit geschlossenen Augen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A simple shape, triangle, circle, square, cross or crescent, is chosen and held in the mind's eye without distortion for as long as possible; only the most determined efforts make it persist. At first the image is sought with closed eyes; with practice it can be projected onto any blank surface. Each day's formal work and extra opportunities are recorded, and no page of the diary is to be left blank."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Image Concentration, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPictorialMindControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical control of pictorial thought, and the projected image as the basis of sigils and thought forms"@en, "Magische Herrschaft über das bildhafte Denken, und das projizierte Bild als Grundlage von Sigillen und Gedankenformen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The part of the mind in which pictorial thoughts arise is said to be brought under magical control by image concentration, the imagined form coming with practice to be projectable onto any blank surface; the technique is called the basis of casting sigils and of creating independent thought forms."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Image Concentration, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicImageConcentrationYieldsPictorialControl a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Image concentration is said to bring pictorial thought under magical control"@en, "Die Bildkonzentration soll das bildhafte Denken unter magische Herrschaft bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Holding the shape without distortion brings the pictorial part of the mind under magical control and lets the form be projected onto a blank surface."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicImageConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPictorialMindControl ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Image Concentration, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalDiary a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical diary: a full page a day, recording time, duration and degree of success of every practice"@en, "Das magische Tagebuch: eine ganze Seite am Tag, mit Zeit, Dauer und Grad des Erfolgs jeder Übung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical diary is called the magician's most essential and powerful tool, large enough for a full page each day. The student records the time, duration and degree of success of any practice and notes environmental factors favourable or not; every day's formal work is recorded and no page left blank; all works of metamorphosis go into it; and significant successes in dreaming are transferred to it from the separate dream record. Though one may come to fear the sight of it, a properly kept record is called the surest guarantor of success in the work of Liber MMM, as a work of reference by which to evaluate progress and as a goad to further effort."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that the record guarantees success is entered here and not as a yielding: the success it guarantees is that of the whole syllabus and not a capacity of its own. The diary records degree of success per practice, which gives it, unlike the record in some later works, a column in which a failure can be written."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, introduction, p. 13; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Mind Control, Image Concentration, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMMMBeforeInitiate a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Success with the student's techniques is a prerequisite for real progress in the initiate syllabus"@en, "Erfolg mit den Techniken des Schülers ist Voraussetzung für echten Fortschritt im Lehrplan des Initiaten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Success with the techniques of Liber MMM, magical trance, metamorphosis and the basic techniques of magic, is called a prerequisite for any real progress with the initiate syllabus of the third degree."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicInitiateSyllabuses ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalTrance ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without success in the studentship techniques there is no real progress with the initiate syllabus." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, introduction, p. 13; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMetamorphosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metamorphosis: willed restructuring of the mind toward the True Will, and the duality every virtue brings"@en, "Metamorphose: gewollte Umgestaltung des Geistes auf den Wahren Willen hin, und die Dualität, die jede Tugend mit sich bringt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmutation of the mind to magical consciousness, often called the Great Work, is said to lead eventually to the discovery of the True Will; even a slight ability to change oneself is valued above any power over the external universe. Every attempt to reorganise the mind involves a duality between the present and the preferred condition, so no virtue, spontaneity, joy, pious pride, grace or omnipotence, can be cultivated without more conventionality, sorrow, guilt, sin and impotence. High magic accepts the dualistic condition and seeks to achieve any arbitrary perceptual perspective at will. Two states are given as objectives, Laughter/Laughter and Non-attachment/Non-disinterest, the first as an antidote to the imbalance and possible madness of the magical trance, the second as a specific against obsession with the magical practices; they are attained by ongoing meditation, entering into the spirit of the condition whenever possible and thinking about it at other times, until a strong new mental habit is set up."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets itself against religions, which it says are founded on the fallacy that one can or ought to have one pole of a duality without the other. Entered at the claim rather than as a dispute: no religious text is cited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, pp. 16-17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicArbitraryPerspective a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Achieving any arbitrary perceptual perspective at will"@en, "Jede beliebige Wahrnehmungsperspektive nach Willen einnehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aim set for high magic: not to choose between the bitter and the sweet of life but to achieve any arbitrary perceptual perspective at will."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicLaughterMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Laughter/Laughter: seeking laughter in what delights, what is neutral, and what is horrific"@en, "Lachen/Lachen: das Lachen suchen im Erfreulichen, im Gleichgültigen und im Grauenhaften"@de ;
    skos:definition "Laughter is called the highest emotion, containing all others from ecstasy to grief and having no opposite, crying being an underdeveloped form of it; it is the only tenable attitude in a universe that is a joke played upon itself. The student seeks the emotion of laughter at what delights and amuses, at whatever is neutral or meaningless, and even at what is horrific and revolting; though forced at first, one can learn to smile inwardly at all things. The Alphabet of Desire returns to it as the amoral cultivation of laughter, and the ecstasy of the death posture closes with laughter evoked at everything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, pp. 83-84; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInwardLaughter a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Smiling inwardly at all things, the antidote to the imbalance of the magical trance"@en, "Innerlich über alles lächeln, das Gegenmittel gegen das Ungleichgewicht der magischen Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition the first meditation aims at: seeing the joke of the universe played out even in neutral and ghastly events and smiling inwardly at all things; it is given as the antidote to the imbalance and possible madness of the magical trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLaughterMeditationYieldsInwardLaughter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ongoing meditation on laughter is said to set up the habit of inward laughter"@en, "Fortgesetzte Meditation über das Lachen soll die Gewohnheit des inneren Lachens einrichten"@de ;
    skos:definition "By entering into the spirit of laughter whenever possible and thinking of it at other times, a strong new mental habit is said to be established; though forced at first, the smile at all things can be learned."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLaughterMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInwardLaughter ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNonAttachmentMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Non-attachment/Non-disinterest: touching one's reality more lightly without losing interest in it"@en, "Nicht-Anhaften/Nicht-Desinteresse: die eigene Wirklichkeit leichter berühren, ohne das Interesse an ihr zu verlieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second meditation. Attachment, and aversion as its other face, to any attribute of oneself, personality, ambitions, relationships or sensory experiences proves limiting; yet it is fatal to lose interest in these things, which are one's symbolic system or magical reality. One tries to touch the sensitive parts of one's reality more lightly, to deny the spoiling hand of grasping desire and boredom, by the same ongoing meditation as for laughter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, pp. 17-18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNonAttachment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Acting without lust of result: doing a thing purely for its own sake with single-pointed awareness"@en, "Handeln ohne Gier nach Ergebnis: etwas rein um seiner selbst willen tun, mit einspitziger Aufmerksamkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Non-attachment/Non-disinterest is said best to describe the magical condition of acting without lust of result. Deciding on something and doing it purely for its own sake is called very difficult for humans, yet precisely this is required to execute magical acts: only single-pointed awareness will do, and it gives enough freedom to act magically. It is the specific against obsession with the magical practices. Liber NOX returns to it: the wizard who knows nothing to be more important than anything else is free to act as though it mattered, and acting without lust of result achieves his will."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, pp. 17-18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, pp. 66-67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNonAttachmentMeditationYieldsNonAttachment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ongoing meditation is said to give the freedom to act without lust of result"@en, "Fortgesetzte Meditation soll die Freiheit geben, ohne Gier nach Ergebnis zu handeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Touching one's reality more lightly denies grasping desire and boredom their hold, and thereby, the work says, one may gain enough freedom to act magically."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicNonAttachmentMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicNonAttachment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, pp. 17-18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTranceMadnessCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The magical trance carries imbalance and possible madness, and the magical practices obsession"@en, "Die magische Trance birgt Ungleichgewicht und möglichen Wahnsinn, die magischen Praktiken Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two meditations of metamorphosis are chosen against two named dangers: the imbalance and possible madness of the magical trance, against which laughter is the antidote, and obsession with the magical practices of the following section, against which non-attachment is the specific."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 17; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLosingInterestCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "It is fatal to lose interest in one's attachments, which are one's magical reality"@en, "Es ist verhängnisvoll, das Interesse an den eigenen Bindungen zu verlieren, die die eigene magische Wirklichkeit sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Attachment or aversion to any attribute of oneself proves limiting, but losing interest in these things is called fatal, since they are one's symbolic system or magical reality; they are to be touched more lightly, not abandoned."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the text against a misreading of its own instruction: non-attachment taken as indifference."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicNonAttachmentMeditation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicHabitMetamorphosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Deleting one minor habit at random and adopting a new one at random"@en, "Eine kleine Gewohnheit nach Zufall ablegen und eine neue nach Zufall annehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third, more active form of metamorphosis works on everyday habits, which in thought, word and deed are called the anchor of the personality that the magician aims to pull up so as to cast himself free on the seas of chaos. The student selects any minor habit at random and deletes it, and at the same time adopts any new habit at random; the choices are to carry no spiritual, egocentric or emotional significance, and nothing with any possibility of failure is to be selected. All works of metamorphosis are committed to the diary."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule that nothing with a possibility of failure may be chosen keeps every attempt of this exercise from failing; it is the only exercise in the book designed so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCapableOfAnything a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming capable of virtually anything by persisting with small changes of habit"@en, "Durch beharrliche kleine Gewohnheitsänderungen zu nahezu allem fähig werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "By persisting with such simple beginnings as the random exchange of minor habits, the student is said to become capable of virtually anything, the personality's anchor in habit having been pulled up."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHabitMetamorphosisYieldsCapableOfAnything a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Persisting with random changes of habit is said to make one capable of virtually anything"@en, "Beharrlicher zufälliger Gewohnheitswechsel soll zu nahezu allem fähig machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By persisting with such simple beginnings you become capable, the work says, of virtually anything."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicHabitMetamorphosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCapableOfAnything ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 18; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTranceBeforeMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Success with ritual, sigils and dreaming depends on some mastery of the trances and of metamorphosis"@en, "Erfolg mit Ritual, Sigillen und Träumen hängt von einiger Beherrschung der Trancen und der Metamorphose ab"@de ;
    skos:definition "Success in the magic part of the studentship syllabus is said to depend on some degree of mastery of the magical trances and of metamorphosis."@en ;
    skos:note "The dependent step named is sigil work, standing for the three techniques of the section, ritual, sigils and dreaming; the prior step metamorphosis is the second half of the same sentence (pmc:ChaosMagicMetamorphosis)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalTrance ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without some mastery of the magical trances and of metamorphosis there is no success in ritual, sigils and dreaming." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDivinationSystem a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Familiarity with one system of divination, used to silence the mind and let inspiration answer"@en, "Vertrautheit mit einem Divinationssystem, gebraucht, um den Geist zu beruhigen und die Eingebung antworten zu lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The student should make himself familiar with at least one system of divination, cards, crystal gazing, runesticks, pendulum or divining rod, the methods being endless. With all of them the aim is to silence the mind and let inspiration supply some sort of answer; whatever instruments are used act only as a receptacle or amplifier for inner abilities. No divinatory system should involve too much randomness, and astrology is not recommended."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitual a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual as weapons, gesture, visualised sigils, word spells and magical trance together"@en, "Ritual als Waffen, Geste, visualisierte Sigillen, Wortzauber und magische Trance zusammen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual is defined as a combination of talismanic weapons, gesture, visualised sigils, word spells and magical trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The banishing ritual of the student: barrier, focus, power symbols, and a brief stop of thought"@en, "Das Bannritual des Schülers: Schranke, Fokus, Machtzeichen und ein kurzes Anhalten des Denkens"@de ;
    skos:definition "First a magical weapon is acquired, a sword, dagger, wand or large ring, impressive to the mind and representing the magician's aspirations; hand forging it, or discovering it in some strange way, is greatly recommended. The ritual contains at least four elements. The magician describes a barrier about himself with the weapon and strongly visualises it, three-dimensional figures being preferred. He focuses his will on a visualised image, the weapon, an imaginary third eye or a ball of light in his head, or uses a sound concentration. He reinforces the barrier with power symbols drawn with the weapon, the pentagram, the eight-pointed star of Chaos or another form, and may use words of power. He aspires to the infinite void by a brief but determined effort to stop thinking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReadinessForConcentration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Being made ready for magical concentration faster than by the trance exercises alone"@en, "Schneller zur magischen Konzentration bereit gemacht werden als durch die Tranceübungen allein"@de ;
    skos:definition "A well-constructed banishing ritual is said to prepare the magician for magical concentration more rapidly than any of the trance exercises alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicResistingObsession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Resisting obsession when dream experiences or sigils becoming conscious cause problems"@en, "Der Besessenheit widerstehen, wenn Traumerfahrungen oder bewusst werdende Sigillen Schwierigkeiten machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The banishing ritual is said to enable the magician to resist obsession if problems are met with dream experiences or with sigils becoming conscious."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOccultProtection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Protection from hostile occult influences"@en, "Schutz vor feindlichen okkulten Einflüssen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The banishing ritual is also said to protect the magician from any hostile occult influences that may assail him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingYieldsReadiness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The banishing ritual is said to prepare for concentration faster than trance exercises"@en, "Das Bannritual soll schneller als Tranceübungen auf die Konzentration vorbereiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first of three effects the work gives a well-constructed banishing ritual."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicReadinessForConcentration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingYieldsResistingObsession a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The banishing ritual is said to let the magician resist obsession"@en, "Das Bannritual soll den Magier der Besessenheit widerstehen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second effect: resistance to obsession from dreams or from sigils that have become conscious."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicResistingObsession ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingYieldsProtection a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The banishing ritual is said to protect from hostile occult influences"@en, "Das Bannritual soll vor feindlichen okkulten Einflüssen schützen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third effect: protection against any hostile occult influence."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicOccultProtection ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingBeforeSigils a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "An effective banishing ritual must be developed before sigils or dreaming"@en, "Ein wirksames Bannritual muss vor Sigillen und Träumen entwickelt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before proceeding with sigils or dreaming it is called essential to develop an effective banishing ritual."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order: the work calls the step essential but names no consequence of skipping it beyond what the ritual is said to protect against. Dreaming is the second dependent step of the same sentence (pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTopic)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWeaponBeforeBanishing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical weapon is acquired before the banishing ritual is developed"@en, "Die magische Waffe wird erworben, bevor das Bannritual entwickelt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "To develop a banishing ritual, a magical weapon is first acquired."@en ;
    skos:note "The weapon has no node of its own; the prior step is the ritual whose first instruction it is, and the dependent step the first of its yieldings. Entered so that the order the work states is on record, at the weakest strength the scale offers above presentation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingYieldsReadiness ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicBanishingRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRequiredCoincidence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bringing about a required coincidence by direct intervention of the will"@en, "Einen benötigten Zufall durch unmittelbares Eingreifen des Willens herbeiführen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the magician requires something he cannot obtain through normal channels, it is sometimes possible to bring about the required coincidence by direct intervention of the will, provided this does not put too great a strain on the universe. The mere act of wanting is rarely effective: the will enters a dialogue with the mind, the desire becomes part of the ego complex, the mind grows anxious of failure, a will not to fulfil the desire arises to reduce that fear, and soon the original desire is a mass of conflicting ideas. The wished-for result often arises only when it has been forgotten, which is called the key to sigils and most forms of spell: sigils work because they stimulate the will to work subconsciously, bypassing the mind."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure are stated: too great a strain on the universe, and mere wanting, which dilutes magical ability. Neither is given a measure; how great a strain is too great is not said, so an unanswered sigil can always be assigned to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 20; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Constructing a sigil: a glyph of desire by the word, the pictorial or the mantrical method"@en, "Eine Sigille bilden: eine Glyphe des Begehrens nach der Wort-, der Bild- oder der Mantramethode"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation of a sigil has three parts: the sigil is constructed, lost to the mind, and charged. In construction the aim is a glyph of desire stylised so as not to suggest the desire at once; complex symbol systems are unnecessary. Figure 2 shows three methods: the word method, writing the statement of desire, eliminating repeated letters and rearranging the rest into a pictorial sigil; the pictorial method, drawn from images; and the mantrical method, rearranging the remaining letters into a chant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, pp. 20-22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExampleSigilSpells a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The spells of the examples: obtaining a book, restraining an adversary, meeting a succubus in dream"@en, "Die Zauber der Beispiele: ein Buch erlangen, einen Gegner hemmen, im Traum einer Sukkuba begegnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three sigils of figure 2 name three aims, one per method: obtaining the Necronomicon by the word method, restraining an adversary by the pictorial method, and meeting a succubus in dream by the mantrical method."@en ;
    skos:note "The work adds that the subject matter of these spells is arbitrary and not recommended. They are entered because each names an effect for a procedure; the work does not report that any was worked."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, pp. 21-22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSigilYieldsExampleSpells a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The sigils of figure 2 are presented as spells for their three aims"@en, "Die Sigillen der Abbildung 2 werden als Zauber für ihre drei Ziele vorgeführt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Each of the three methods is shown worked on a statement of desire, the sigil standing as the spell for that desire."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicExampleSigilSpells ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, pp. 21-22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSigilYieldsRequiredCoincidence a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The sigil is said to bring about the required coincidence by working the will subconsciously"@en, "Die Sigille soll den benötigten Zufall herbeiführen, indem sie den Willen unbewusst wirken lässt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sigils are said to work because they stimulate the will to work subconsciously, bypassing the mind, which the work takes from the observation that results often arise only once forgotten."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSigilConstruction ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicRequiredCoincidence ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 20; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicLosingTheSigil a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Losing the sigil: banishing form and desire from waking consciousness"@en, "Die Sigille verlieren: Form und Begehren aus dem wachen Bewusstsein verbannen"@de ;
    skos:definition "To lose the sigil successfully both its form and the associated desire must be banished from normal waking consciousness; the magician strives against any manifestation of either by forcefully turning his attention to other matters. The sigil may be burnt, buried or cast into an ocean. A word spell can be lost by constant repetition, which in the end empties the mind of the associated desire. A record is kept of all sigil work, but not so as to cause conscious deliberation over the sigilised desire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicChargingTheSigil a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Charging the sigil at moments of trance or of paralysing emotion, then banishing it with laughter"@en, "Die Sigille laden in Augenblicken der Trance oder lähmender Erregung, dann sie mit Lachen bannen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sigil is charged when the mind has reached quiescence through magical trance, or when high emotionality paralyses its normal functioning; it is then concentrated upon as a mental image, a mantra or a drawn form. The moments named are magical trance; orgasm or great elation; great fear, anger or embarrassment; and intense frustration or disappointment. Alternatively, when another strong desire arises it is sacrificed, that is forgotten, and the sigil concentrated on instead. After holding the sigil as long as possible it is wise to banish it by evoking laughter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLosingBeforeCharging a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The sigil is constructed, lost to the mind, and only then charged"@en, "Die Sigille wird gebildet, dem Geist verloren und erst dann geladen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three parts of the operation are given in order: construction, loss to the mind, charging."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicChargingTheSigil ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicLosingTheSigil ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 20; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSigilRecordCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Record sigil work, but never so as to deliberate consciously over the desire"@en, "Die Sigillenarbeit aufzeichnen, aber nie so, dass man bewusst über das Begehren nachdenkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A record should be kept of all work with sigils, but not in such a way as to cause conscious deliberation over the sigilised desire."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicLosingTheSigil ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, Sigils, p. 22; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamPlaneControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Full access to the dream plane and control of it, the egress to divination, entities and exteriorisation"@en, "Voller Zugang zur Traumebene und Herrschaft über sie, der Ausgang zu Divination, Wesenheiten und Exteriorisation"@de ;
    skos:definition "The dream state is called a convenient egress into the fields of divination, of entities, and of exteriorisation or out-of-body experience. All humans dream nightly but few can recount their dreams minutes after waking, the brain having learned to keep such incongruous experience from waking consciousness; the magician aims to gain full access to the dream plane and to assume control of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDreamRecord a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The dream record: book and pen beside the bed, every dream written down on waking"@en, "Die Traumaufzeichnung: Buch und Stift neben dem Bett, jeder Traum beim Erwachen niedergeschrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A book and writing instrument are kept next to the place of sleeping at all times, and the details of all dreams are recorded as soon as possible after waking. The dream record is kept apart from the magical diary, since it grows voluminous, and significant successes are transferred to the diary."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTopic a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Setting up a dream by visualising its topic in a silenced mind immediately before sleep"@en, "Einen Traum einrichten, indem man sein Thema unmittelbar vor dem Schlaf in einem stillen Geist visualisiert"@de ;
    skos:definition "To assume conscious control of dreaming a topic is selected. The dream is set up by strongly visualising the desired topic in an otherwise silenced mind immediately before sleep; for more complex experiences the method of sigils may be used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamTopicYieldsDreamControl a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Visualising a topic before sleep is said to give conscious control over the dream"@en, "Ein vor dem Schlaf visualisiertes Thema soll bewusste Herrschaft über den Traum geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work presents the visualised topic as the way to assume conscious control over the dream state."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTopic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDreamPlaneControl ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamRecordBeforeAccess a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The dream record is the only method of gaining full access to the dream plane"@en, "Die Traumaufzeichnung ist die einzige Methode, vollen Zugang zur Traumebene zu gewinnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The only method of gaining full access to the dream plane is said to be keeping a book by the bed and recording all dreams on waking."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicDreamPlaneControl ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicDreamRecord ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the record there is no full access to the dream plane, since it is called the only method." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSimpleDreamsFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Simple dream experiences are mastered before divination or exteriorisation in dream"@en, "Einfache Traumerfahrungen werden gemeistert, bevor Divination oder Exteriorisation im Traum versucht wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician should start with simple experiences, such as the desire to see a particular object, real or imaginary, and master this before attempting divination or exteriorisation."@en ;
    skos:note "Both steps fall within the same practice; the order is entered at the yielding that names the full capacity, for want of a separate node for the first exercise."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicDreamTopicYieldsDreamControl ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTopic ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicCensorBattleCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Taking control of dreams means a deadly and bizarre battle with one's own psychic censor"@en, "Die Herrschaft über die Träume bedeutet einen tödlichen und bizarren Kampf mit dem eigenen psychischen Zensor"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attempt to gain full access to the dream plane and control of it is said invariably to involve the magician in a deadly and bizarre battle with his own psychic censor, which will use almost any tactics to deny him these experiences."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicDreamPlaneControl ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInitiateSyllabuses a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The initiate syllabuses: white magic for wisdom, black magic for power, and no middle way"@en, "Die Lehrpläne des Initiaten: weiße Magie für Weisheit, schwarze Magie für Macht, und kein mittlerer Weg"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magic art may be divided in many ways; the division favoured is temperamental. White magic, the syllabus of Liber LUX, leans toward the acquisition of wisdom and a general faith in the universe; black magic, the syllabus of Liber NOX, toward the acquisition of power and a basic faith in oneself. The end results are said to be not dissimilar, since the paths meet in a way impossible to describe, and initiates may work from either or both."@en ;
    skos:note "The so-called middle way or path of knowledge, the acquisition of secondhand ideas, is rejected as an excuse to do neither that leads nowhere. Entered at the claim: the work names no holder of that way and cites no text."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Initiate Syllabuses, Liber LUX and Liber NOX, pp. 24-25; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDuality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Duality: the senses perceive only differences, and the thinking mind splits everything in two"@en, "Dualität: die Sinne nehmen nur Unterschiede wahr, und der denkende Verstand spaltet alles in zwei"@de ;
    skos:definition "Duality is called humanity's usual condition: happiness exists only because of misery, pain because of comfort, good because of evil, existence because of non-existence. All phenomena must be paired, since the senses are equipped to perceive only differences, and the thinking mind, being dualistic itself, splits everything it meets into two. It also sees a world of being and doing, and so of cause and effect, which cannot explain how anything exists in the first place."@en ;
    skos:note "Liber LUX opens with its schema, figure 3: the Augoeides above Divination, Enchantment, Invocation and Evocation, with the theory of Chaos, Kia, Duality, Aether and Mind. The order of the syllabus follows it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, pp. 27-28; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kia: the unity that appears to the mind as will and perception, a fragment of the life force of the universe"@en, "Kia: die Einheit, die dem Verstand als Wille und Wahrnehmung erscheint, ein Bruchstück der Lebenskraft des Universums"@de ;
    skos:definition "Man considers himself a centre of will and of perception, which only appear separate to the mind; the unity that appears to exert both is called Kia by magicians, sometimes spirit, soul or life force. It cannot be experienced directly, being the basis of consciousness without fixed qualities, the elusive I that confers self-awareness; it can sometimes be felt as ecstasy or inspiration but is mostly trapped in wandering thought, identification with experience and ego. It is a fragment of the great life force of the universe, which holds twin impulses, to immerse itself in duality and to escape from it; Kia will reincarnate until the first is exhausted, and the second is the root of the mystic quest. Liber NOX calls it nascent energy seeking form, the Great Desire, the Life Force or Self-Love, represented by Atu 0, the Fool or the Joker, with the vulture for heraldic beast; among its titles is Anon."@en ;
    skos:note "Magic is said to be concerned with giving Kia more freedom and flexibility and means to manifest its occult power, which it has as a fragment of the life force."@en ;
    skos:note "The author gives in the first person an experience he says he received: that his innermost self was no thing, formless, without quality, nameless pure power, yet whatever it touched. It is his own report and names no other speaker, so no pm:Receiving was entered for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, pp. 28-29; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, pp. 57-60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos: what originates and keeps events happening, called so because the name is nearly meaningless"@en, "Chaos: was die Ereignisse hervorbringt und am Laufen hält, so genannt, weil der Name nahezu bedeutungslos ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The thing responsible for the origin and continued action of events is called Chaos by magicians; it could as well be called God or Tao, but the name Chaos is said to be virtually meaningless and free of the anthropomorphic ideas of religion. Chaos is also the force that adds increasing complexity to the universe by spawning structures not inherent in their parts, that made life evolve out of dust, and that is most concentratedly manifest in the human life force, Kia, as the source of consciousness. Liber AOM adds that out of it arise two prime forces, the light power and the dark (pmc:ChaosMagicLightAndDarkPowers)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, p. 28; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAether a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The aether: the half-formed realm of possibilities through which Chaos becomes real effects"@en, "Der Äther: das halbgeformte Reich der Möglichkeiten, durch das Chaos zu wirklichen Wirkungen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Between Chaos and ordinary matter, and between Kia and the mind, lies a realm of half-formed substance called aether: dualistic but tenuous and probabilistic, consisting of all the possibilities Chaos throws out that have not yet become solid realities, the medium by which non-existent chaos translates itself into real effects and the backdrop out of which real events and thoughts materialise. Aetheric events may have no precise location, mass or energy and so need not affect the physical plane. Its normal function is as an intermediary between Kia and thought, and its properties are so mutable that almost anything can be accomplished with it: thought gives it shape and Kia gives it power. All magical systems, stripped of local symbolism, are said to show a uniform method because all derive from shamanism."@en ;
    skos:note "The work says the physical sciences have begun to blunder into the aetheric with quantum indeterminacy and virtual processes in subatomic matter. It is the earliest place in the corpus where a magical medium is identified with quantum indeterminacy; the later model of The Apophenion (pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum) puts the quantum into the brain instead."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, pp. 29-30; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicManipulatingTheAether a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Calling unlikely coincidences into existence by manipulating the aether, and extending will and perception beyond the body"@en, "Unwahrscheinliche Zufälle durch Handhabung des Äthers ins Dasein rufen, und Wille und Wahrnehmung über den Körper hinaus ausdehnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the aetheric realm of possibility only sensible, causal, probable events usually come into existence; but as centres of Kia or Chaos ourselves we can sometimes call very unlikely coincidences or unexpected events into existence by manipulating the aether, and such is magic. Thus will and perception are said to be extended into areas of time and space beyond the physical limits of the body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, p. 29; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUnionOfKiaWithChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kia becoming one with Chaos, and so extending its will and perception into the universe"@en, "Kia wird eins mit dem Chaos und dehnt so Wille und Wahrnehmung ins Universum aus"@de ;
    skos:definition "To the extent that Kia can become one with Chaos it can extend its will and perception into the universe to accomplish magic. The mind must finally be surrendered as one enters fully into Chaos, and a complete and balanced psychocosm is said to be more easily surrendered; enchantment rests on the same identification, the magician changing an event only by matching the Chaos that upholds it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, introduction, p. 29; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 41; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis: the one-pointed consciousness reached by inhibition or by excitation"@en, "Gnosis: das einspitzige Bewusstsein, erreicht durch Hemmung oder durch Erregung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Altered states of consciousness are called the key to magical powers. The state required has a name in every tradition, no-mind, stopping the internal dialogue, passing through the eye of the needle, ain, samadhi, one-pointedness, and is here called gnosis, an extension of the magical trance by other means. In the inhibitory mode the mind is progressively silenced until a single object of concentration remains; in the excitatory mode it is raised to a high pitch of excitement while concentration on the objective is held, and strong stimulation at last elicits a reflex inhibition that paralyses all but the object. Both end in the same effect. Table 1 sets the methods side by side: death posture against sexual excitation; magical trance concentration against emotional arousal by fear, anger and horror; sleeplessness against pain and torture; fasting against flagellation; exhaustion against dancing, drumming and chanting; gazing against the right way of walking; hypnotic or trance-inducing drugs against excitatory or disinhibitory drugs, mild hallucinogens and forced over-breathing; sensory deprivation against sensory overload. Certain forms suit some kinds of magic better than others, and the initiate adapts them by his own ingenium. Liber NOX calls gnosis the mechanism by which Kia draws back from the flesh in preparation for the indulgences of magic, a great saving for a greater spending."@en ;
    skos:note "The work says neurophysiology has finally stumbled on what magicians have known by experience for millennia, and quotes an unnamed great master on the two methods of becoming god, the upright and the averse. Neither the neurophysiology nor the master is cited by work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 31; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, p. 60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefImplantation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Implanting beliefs during gnosis, by the hypersuggestibility of the vacuous mind"@en, "Glaubenssätze in der Gnosis einpflanzen, durch die Übersuggestibilität des leeren Geistes"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is during the moments of single-pointed concentration, gnosis, that beliefs can be implanted for magic and the life force induced to manifest. The Alphabet of Desire repeats the claim: the gnostic condition is the key to radical changes of belief or conversion, since any belief presented in it is likely to be retained by the hypersuggestibility of the vacuous mind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 31; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The death posture: a feint at death that negates thought, by stopping the breath or by the mirror gaze"@en, "Die Todeshaltung: ein Scheintod, der das Denken aufhebt, durch Anhalten des Atems oder durch den Spiegelblick"@de ;
    skos:definition "A feint at death for an utter negation of thought, ranging from the simple not-thinking exercise to complex rituals. The fast form blocks ears, nose and mouth and covers the eyes with the hands, jamming back breath and thoughts until near unconsciousness involuntarily breaks the posture. In the mirror form one sits about two feet from a mirror and stares fixedly with an unblinking, corpselike gaze at the image of one's own eyes. Liber NOX gives it as the first of the Rites of Thanateros: kneeling in the dragon position, hands flat on the thighs, spine erect, in a featureless black or white temple, perhaps after one of the magical trances, the initiate stares at his eyes in a large mirror and stops thinking; no effort in the usual sense avails, infinite patience barely suffices, and any distortion of the image is a symptom of thinking. Then the eyes close and the void is entered as completely as possible, a visualised shape serving as receptacle of thought until it too fades and leaves Kia hovering in an immensity. In the Alphabet of Desire it covers all trances meant to bring the mind to complete stillness, concentration on a single stimulus hastening the removal of others."@en ;
    skos:note "Signs of success, stated with a disclaimer: loss of physical perspective or body image, the body feeling vast or microscopic. The work says they are characteristic of sensory deprivation, useless to strive for, and not the desired result but indicative of a loosening of belief. The further sign is Kia left hovering in an immensity. Condition of failure: any distortion of the mirror image is symptomatic of thinking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 31; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 68-69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathPostureYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The death posture is said to silence the mind and leave Kia hovering in the void"@en, "Die Todeshaltung soll den Geist zum Schweigen bringen und Kia in der Leere schweben lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effort to keep an absolutely unwavering image is said of itself to silence the mind after a while; with the eyes closed and the visualised receptacle faded, Kia is left hovering in an immensity. The endless variations of the gnosis of quiescence are all called the death posture."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 31; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 68-69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSexualGnosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual excitation as gnosis, the lust transferred to the matter of the working"@en, "Sexuelle Erregung als Gnosis, die Lust auf den Gegenstand der Arbeit übertragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sexual excitation can be obtained by any preferred method; in all cases there has to be a transference from the lust that ignites the sexuality to the matter of the magical working. Its nature lends itself to the creation of independent beings, evocation; in invocation the union sought can be mirrored on the physical plane, the partner being visualised as an incarnation of the desired idea or god. Prolonged excitement through karezza, inhibition of orgasm or repeated orgasmic collapse can lead to trance states useful for divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 31-33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualGnosisYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual excitation is listed among the excitatory methods that reach gnosis"@en, "Sexuelle Erregung steht unter den erregenden Methoden, die zur Gnosis führen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Table 1 sets sexual excitation against the death posture at the head of the two modes, both ending in one-pointed consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSexualGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 31-33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualGnosisYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Prolonged sexual excitement is said to lead to trance states useful for divination"@en, "Lange sexuelle Erregung soll zu Trancezuständen führen, die für die Divination taugen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prolonged sexual excitement through karezza, inhibition of orgasm or repeated orgasmic collapse can lead to trance states useful for divination."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSexualGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRegainingSexuality a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Regaining one's original sexuality by abstention and by arousing lust without fantasy"@en, "Die ursprüngliche Sexualität durch Enthaltung und durch Erregung ohne Phantasie zurückgewinnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "It may be necessary to regain one's original sexuality from the mass of fantasy and association into which it mostly sinks; this is done by judicious use of abstention and by arousing lust without any mental prop or fantasy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOriginalSexuality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "One's original sexuality, freed from fantasy and association"@en, "Die ursprüngliche Sexualität, befreit von Phantasie und Assoziation"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sexuality recovered from the fantasy and association into which it mostly sinks; the exercise that recovers it is also called therapeutic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRegainingSexualityYieldsOriginalSexuality a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Abstention and lust without fantasy are said to regain the original sexuality"@en, "Enthaltung und Lust ohne Phantasie sollen die ursprüngliche Sexualität zurückgewinnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Judicious abstention and arousal without mental prop are said to achieve it, and the exercise to be therapeutic as well."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRegainingSexuality ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicOriginalSexuality ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEmotionalArousal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional arousal: anger, fear and horror raised until they paralyse the mind, the objective kept in view"@en, "Emotionale Erregung: Zorn, Furcht und Grauen gesteigert, bis sie den Geist lähmen, das Ziel im Blick"@de ;
    skos:definition "The obverse of the concentrations of magical trance. Any emotion can in theory serve, even love or grief in extreme circumstances, but in practice only anger, fear and horror are easily generated strongly enough. The well-known ability of fear and anger to paralyse the mind indicates their effectiveness, yet the magician must never lose sight of the objectives of his working. The Alphabet of Desire adds that fear pushed rapidly to a high pitch paralyses the mind, that strange alternative perceptions may then be glimpsed and the will, focused on one objective, becomes mighty, and that terror is an essential ingredient of many initiatory schools."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 80; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEmotionalArousalYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Over-stimulated emotion is said to paralyse the mind into catharsis, ecstasy and one-pointedness"@en, "Übersteigerte Emotion soll den Geist bis zu Katharsis, Ekstase und Einspitzigkeit lähmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fear and anger paralyse the mind, which indicates their effectiveness; over-stimulation of any emotive function is said to create a paroxysm experienced as catharsis, at higher levels as ecstasy, and finally as the one-pointed consciousness in which the life force can act directly."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEmotionalArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIdiocyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Arousal must never reduce the magician to gibbering idiocy or catatonia"@en, "Die Erregung darf den Magier nie zu stammelndem Schwachsinn oder Katatonie herabsetzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician must never lose sight of the objectives of his working; nothing is to be gained and much may be lost by reducing oneself to gibbering idiocy or catatonia."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEmotionalArousal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMonasticExhaustion a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sleeplessness, fasting and exhaustion, with the mind turned constantly to the object"@en, "Schlaflosigkeit, Fasten und Erschöpfung, der Geist beständig dem Gegenstand zugewandt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sleeplessness, fasting and exhaustion are called old monastic favourites; during them there is to be a constant turning of the mind toward the object of the exercise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMonasticExhaustionYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sleeplessness, fasting and exhaustion are listed among the inhibitory methods of gnosis"@en, "Schlaflosigkeit, Fasten und Erschöpfung stehen unter den hemmenden Methoden der Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Table 1 sets them in the inhibitory column, ending with the others in one-pointed consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMonasticExhaustion ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMonasticExhaustionYieldsPrescience a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sleeplessness, fasting and exhaustion are said to cause prescience through visions"@en, "Schlaflosigkeit, Fasten und Erschöpfung sollen Vorauswissen durch Visionen bewirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "In divination, sleeplessness, fasting and exhaustion may cause prescience through visions, though as with drugs concentration is hard to keep."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMonasticExhaustion ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPrescience ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPainGnosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Pain, torture and flagellation, surrendered to until ecstasy"@en, "Schmerz, Folter und Geißelung, denen man sich bis zur Ekstase hingibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pain, torture and flagellation are said to have been used by witches, monks and fakirs; surrender to pain brings eventual ecstasy and the necessary one-pointedness. In the supplementary alphabet pain is given magical use in rites of initiation, purification and sacrifice, and complete agony is called ecstasy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 86; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPainGnosisYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Surrender to pain is said to bring ecstasy and one-pointedness"@en, "Hingabe an den Schmerz soll Ekstase und Einspitzigkeit bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Surrender to pain brings eventual ecstasy and the necessary one-pointedness; over-stimulation of pain leads to ecstatic states, which are harder to reach through pleasure."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPainGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 86; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPainDamageCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "With high resistance to pain, needless bodily damage may come before the threshold"@en, "Bei hohem Schmerzwiderstand kann vor der Schwelle unnötiger körperlicher Schaden entstehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the organism's resistance to pain is high, needless damage to the body may result before the threshold is crossed."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPainGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDancingDrummingChanting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Dancing, drumming and chanting, arranged to bring the participants to a climax"@en, "Tanzen, Trommeln und Singen, so eingerichtet, dass die Teilnehmer zum Höhepunkt kommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dancing, drumming and chanting require careful arrangement and preparation to bring the participants to a climax; lyrical exaltation through emotive poetry, incantation, song, prayer or supplication can be added, the whole being best controlled by some form of ritual. Over-breathing is sometimes used to supplement dancing or leaping."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDancingYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Dancing, drumming and chanting are listed among the excitatory methods of gnosis"@en, "Tanzen, Trommeln und Singen stehen unter den erregenden Methoden der Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Table 1 places them in the excitatory column, brought to a climax under ritual control."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDancingDrummingChanting ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRightWayOfWalking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The right way of walking: long walks with crossed, unfocused eyes and a peripheral view of everything"@en, "Die rechte Art des Gehens: lange Gänge mit gekreuzten, unscharfen Augen und einem Rundblick auf alles"@de ;
    skos:definition "Not a technique for immediate results but a meditation that helps the mind stop thinking. One walks for long stretches without looking at anything directly, slightly crossing and unfocusing the eyes and keeping a peripheral view of everything, cognisant of the whole 180-degree arc from side to side and from the toes to the sky; the fingers are curled or clasped in unusual positions to draw attention to the arms."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the mind should eventually become totally absorbed in its environment, and thinking will cease."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWalkingYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The right way of walking is said to help the mind stop thinking"@en, "Die rechte Art des Gehens soll dem Geist helfen, das Denken einzustellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A meditation that helps the mind stop thinking; eventually the mind becomes totally absorbed in its environment and thinking ceases."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRightWayOfWalking ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGazing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Gazing: the whole attention on a natural object, the body motionless"@en, "Schauen: die ganze Aufmerksamkeit auf einem natürlichen Gegenstand, der Körper reglos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The inhibitory variant of the right way of walking: the entire attention is directed to the sight of some object in the environment while the body is kept motionless; any natural phenomenon, plants, rocks, sky, water or fire, may be used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGazingYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Gazing is listed among the inhibitory methods of gnosis"@en, "Das Schauen steht unter den hemmenden Methoden der Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Table 1 sets gazing in the inhibitory column against the right way of walking."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGazing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDrugGnosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Drugs in small doses to heighten the excitation of other methods"@en, "Drogen in kleinen Dosen, um die Erregung anderer Methoden zu steigern"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is said to be no magic drug that will by itself have the required effect; drugs can be used in small doses to heighten the effect of excitation caused by the methods already discussed."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated: no drug by itself produces gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, pp. 34-35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDrugDoseCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A large dose leads to depression, confusion and loss of control; inhibitory drugs may sever life force and body"@en, "Eine große Dosis führt zu Niedergeschlagenheit, Verwirrung und Kontrollverlust; hemmende Drogen können Lebenskraft und Körper trennen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In all cases a large dose leads to depression, confusion and a general loss of control. Inhibitory drugs must be considered with even more caution because of their inherent danger: they often simply sever the life force and body altogether."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicDrugGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSensoryOverload a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sensory overload: a battery of techniques used together"@en, "Reizüberflutung: eine ganze Reihe von Techniken zugleich angewandt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensory overload is achieved when a battery of techniques is used together; in the work's example from certain tantric rites the candidate is beaten by his guru, hashish is forced on him, and he is taken at midnight to a dark cemetery for sacred sexual intercourse, and so achieves union with his god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSensoryOverloadYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sensory overload is said to bring the candidate to union with his god"@en, "Reizüberflutung soll den Kandidaten zur Vereinigung mit seinem Gott bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the tantric example the battery of techniques ends in the candidate's union with his god, which the work places in the excitatory column of gnosis."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSensoryOverload ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSensoryDeprivation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sensory deprivation: hoods, blindfolds and earplugs, all input obliterated for a short time"@en, "Reizentzug: Hauben, Augenbinden und Ohrstöpsel, jede Wahrnehmung für kurze Zeit ausgelöscht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensory deprivation is called the essence of the monastic cell, the mountain cave, the walled-up hermit and the rites of death, burial and resurrection; much the same effect is had with hoods, blindfolds, earplugs, repetitive sounds and restricted movement. It is said to be far more effective to obliterate all sensory input completely for a short period than to reduce it over a longer one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSensoryDeprivationYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Short, complete sensory deprivation is said to work better than long reduction"@en, "Kurzer, vollständiger Reizentzug soll besser wirken als lange Verminderung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensory deprivation stands in the inhibitory column; total obliteration of input for a short time is said to be far more effective than reduction over a longer one."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSensoryDeprivation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicModesSimultaneousCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Inhibitory and excitatory techniques may follow each other but not run together in one operation"@en, "Hemmende und erregende Techniken dürfen aufeinander folgen, aber nicht in einer Operation zugleich laufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Inhibitory and excitatory techniques can be employed sequentially, but not simultaneously, in the same operation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Gnosis, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEntities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical beings: portions of Kia attached to aetheric matter, budded off from the mind"@en, "Magische Wesen: Anteile des Kia, an ätherische Materie gebunden, aus dem Geist abgesprosst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Evocation deals with magical beings, which have a legion of names from many demonologies: elementals, familiars, incubi, succubi, bud-wills, demons, automata, atavisms, wraiths, spirits. They may be bound to talismans, places, animals, objects, persons or incense smoke, or move in the aether. They are not limited to obsessions and complexes in the mind: though they usually originate there, they may be budded off and attached to objects and places as ghosts, spirits or vibrations, or act at a distance as fetishes, familiars or poltergeists. They consist of a portion of Kia or life force attached to some aetheric matter, which may or may not be attached to ordinary matter. They come from three sources: those discovered clairvoyantly, those whose characteristics the grimoires give, and those the magician creates."@en ;
    skos:note "Poltergeist activity is said invariably to centre on the sexually disturbed, children at puberty or more rarely women at menopause, intense excitation letting the life force manifest frustration outside the body. The grimoires are said to have been compiled by Catholic priests, much of their content being deliberate abomination in their own terms; no grimoire is cited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, pp. 36-37; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation: creating or binding semi-independent beings to cause change in oneself, others or the universe"@en, "Evokation: halbselbständige Wesen erschaffen oder binden, um Veränderung in sich, in anderen oder im Universum zu bewirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Evocation is the summoning or creation of partial beings to accomplish some purpose: change in oneself, in others or in the universe. A semi-independent being is said to have advantages over direct transformation by will: it goes on fulfilling its function independently of the magician until its life force dissipates, and being semi-sentient it can adapt itself to a task as a simple spell cannot. A created elemental, for instance, may assist with divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, pp. 36-37; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInspirationByPossession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Inspirations, abilities and knowledge received during possession by certain entities"@en, "Eingebungen, Fähigkeiten und Wissen, empfangen während der Besessenheit durch bestimmte Wesenheiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "During moments of possession by certain entities the magician may be the recipient of inspirations, abilities and knowledge not normally accessible to him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 37; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationProcedure a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The procedure of evocation: attributes placed in the mind, charged by gnosis into autonomy, then banishing"@en, "Das Verfahren der Evokation: Eigenschaften in den Geist gelegt, durch Gnosis zur Selbständigkeit geladen, dann Bannung"@de ;
    skos:definition "First the attributes of the entity, its type, scope, name, appearance and characteristics, are placed in the mind or made known to it; automatic drawing or writing, a stylus let move under inspiration across a surface, may uncover the nature of a clairvoyantly discovered being, while for a created being a composite sigil of its desired attributes is assembled, with a name, an image and if desired a number. Second, will and perception are focused as intently as possible by some gnostic method on the being's sigils or characteristics, so that these take on a portion of the magician's life force and begin autonomous existence; with a pre-existing being this binds it to the magician's will. Self-banishing or even exorcism follows to restore normal consciousness. A being of a low order with a single task can be left to fulfil its destiny; to terminate one, its sigil or material basis is destroyed and its mental image destroyed or reabsorbed by visualisation. For more powerful beings conjuration and exorcism must be in proportion to the power of the ritual that evoked them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, pp. 37-39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEvocationProcedureYieldsEvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Charging the being's sigil in gnosis is said to give it autonomous existence"@en, "Das Laden der Sigille des Wesens in der Gnosis soll ihm selbständiges Dasein geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Focusing will and perception by a gnostic method on the being's sigil is said to give it a portion of the magician's life force, so that it begins autonomous existence, or, if it already existed, is bound to his will."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationProcedure ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 37; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisDepthForControl a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Powerful beings may be controlled only by re-entering gnosis to the depth of their evocation"@en, "Mächtige Wesen lassen sich nur beherrschen, indem man die Gnosis in der Tiefe ihrer Evokation wieder betritt"@de ;
    skos:definition "To control more powerful and independent beings the magician may have to re-enter the gnostic state to the same depth as before in order to draw their power; conjuration and exorcism must be in proportion to the power of the original ritual."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, pp. 37-39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishingAfterEvocation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Self-banishing follows the charging, before the magician goes forth"@en, "Die Selbstbannung folgt der Ladung, bevor der Magier hinausgeht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The charging is customarily followed by self-banishing or even exorcism, to restore the magician's consciousness to normal before he goes forth."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an order within the procedure; the work states it as custom and names no consequence."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEvocationProcedureYieldsEvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationProcedure ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 37; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTheurgicRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Theurgic evocation by visualisation, complex ceremonial, prayer and command"@en, "Theurgische Evokation durch Visualisierung, verwickeltes Zeremoniell, Gebet und Befehl"@de ;
    skos:definition "Theurgic ritual depends solely on visualisation and concentration on complex ceremonial to achieve focus, with conjuration by prayer, supplication or command; it can be improved by poetic exaltation, chanting, ecstatic dancing and drumming."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure are stated. Increasing complexity tends to distract rather than focus, the will becomes multiple and the result is often disappointing; and conjuration by prayer, supplication or command is rarely effective unless the appeal is desperate or prolonged until exhaustion. Both name an outcome, the disappointing result, though not how it is to be recognised."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTheurgicRitualYieldsEvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Theurgic ritual is offered as one of the methods of evocation"@en, "Das theurgische Ritual wird als eine der Methoden der Evokation angeboten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any technique of gnosis can in theory be used in evocation; theurgic ritual is the first analysed, effective when the appeal is desperate or prolonged to exhaustion and improved by exaltation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTheurgicRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGoeticTerror a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Goetic evocation by terror: the rite moved to a graveyard or crypt at midnight"@en, "Goetische Evokation durch Schrecken: der Ritus auf einen Friedhof oder in eine Gruft um Mitternacht verlegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Goetic tradition of the grimoires is said to add terror. Transported to a graveyard or crypt at midnight, the rite becomes a powerful mechanism for concentrating Kia by paralysing the peripheral functions of the mind with fear."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success, conditional: if the magician can maintain control under these conditions his will is singular and mighty."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGoeticTerrorYieldsEvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Terror is said to concentrate Kia and make the will singular and mighty for evocation"@en, "Schrecken soll das Kia sammeln und den Willen für die Evokation einzig und mächtig machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fear paralyses the peripheral functions of the mind and so concentrates Kia; if control is kept, the will is singular and mighty."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGoeticTerror ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOphidianEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ophidian evocation: the entity's sigil concentrated on at orgasm and anointed with the sexual fluids"@en, "Ophidische Evokation: die Sigille des Wesens beim Orgasmus konzentriert und mit den Sexualflüssigkeiten gesalbt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Ophidian tradition uses sexual orgasm to focus will and perception: the attributes of an entity in sigilised form are concentrated on at orgasm and may afterwards be anointed with the sex fluids. The process is likened to the deliberate creation of an obsession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOphidianYieldsEvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "With enough power the Ophidian rite is said to give the entity independent existence"@en, "Mit genug Kraft soll der ophidische Ritus dem Wesen selbständiges Dasein geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "If enough power can be put into it, the deliberately created obsession will be capable of independent existence."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOphidianEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIncubiAndSuccubiNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Incubi and succubi, pre-existing beings of other people's pathological sexuality"@en, "Inkuben und Sukkuben, vorhandene Wesen aus der krankhaften Sexualität anderer"@de ;
    skos:definition "Incubi and succubi are named as pre-existing entities created by other peoples' pathological sexuality; incubi traditionally seek intercourse with living women and succubi with men, often in sleep, but both are said to be almost invariably male, succubi making some slight attempt to disguise themselves as female. Both are called predatory and stupid, with little power or motivation for anything but sex."@en ;
    skos:note "A class of beings and not a single name; entered as one naming because the work characterises them together."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "incubi, succubi" ;
    pm:nameRole "pre-existing entity" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, pp. 39-40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBloodSacrifice a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Blood sacrifice in Goetic evocation, best with one's own blood let fall on the demon's sigil"@en, "Blutopfer in der goetischen Evokation, am besten mit dem eigenen Blut, das auf die Sigille des Dämons fällt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sacrifice has been used to create fear or terror, or to invoke the gnosis of pain in support of Goetic evocations. Blood sacrifice is said to be most effective and most easily controlled with one's own blood, customarily allowed to fall upon the sigil or talisman of the demon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSacrificeCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Sacrifice exhausts itself, and the power to control it brings the wisdom to avoid it"@en, "Das Opfer erschöpft sich, und die Macht, es zu beherrschen, bringt die Weisheit, es zu meiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sacrifice easily exhausts itself, and the sorcerer may end up wading in oceans of blood, as the Aztecs did, for very little result; the power to control blood sacrifice usually brings with it the wisdom to avoid it in favour of other methods."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with its reason: the method exhausts itself and gives very little result."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicBloodSacrifice ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVisibleAppearance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuring a demon to visible appearance in incense smoke, after fasting, sleep and sensory deprivation"@en, "Einen Dämon im Weihrauchqualm zur sichtbaren Erscheinung beschwören, nach Fasten, Schlaf und Reizentzug"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beings do not normally have a physical appearance and have to be persuaded to assume one. Fasting, sleep and sensory deprivation combined with drugs and clouds of incense smoke will usually give a demon a sufficiently sensitive and malleable medium in which to manifest an image if commanded to do so."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDemonImage a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A demon manifesting an image when commanded, in a malleable medium"@en, "Ein Dämon, der auf Befehl in einem formbaren Medium ein Bild hervorbringt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Given a sensitive and malleable medium a demon will usually manifest an image if commanded; the work adds at once that such appearances are hallucinations, since these beings do not normally have a physical appearance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVisibleAppearanceYieldsDemonImage a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Fasting, sensory deprivation, drugs and incense are said to let a commanded demon manifest an image"@en, "Fasten, Reizentzug, Drogen und Weihrauch sollen einen befohlenen Dämon ein Bild hervorbringen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The combination will usually provide a demon with media sensitive and malleable enough to manifest an image when commanded."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicVisibleAppearance ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDemonImage ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVisibleAppearanceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "To conjure spirits to visible appearance as proof of their reality is ill-considered"@en, "Geister zum Beweis ihrer Wirklichkeit sichtbar zu beschwören ist unbedacht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conjuration to visible appearance to prove to oneself or others the objective reality of spirits is called an ill-considered act: the conditions needed for the appearance will always allow the belief that it is the result of hypnosis, hallucination or delusion, and indeed it is a hallucination."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission states in advance that the appearance cannot discriminate: its conditions always admit the other explanation. Recorded as a finding about the claim, not counted as a condition of failure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicVisibleAppearance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReabsorbingDemons a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "All one's thoughts, obsessions and demons must be reabsorbed before Kia can become one with Chaos"@en, "Alle eigenen Gedanken, Besessenheiten und Dämonen müssen zurückgenommen sein, bevor Kia eins mit dem Chaos werden kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "The medieval idea of a pact is called an over-dramatisation with a germ of truth: all one's thoughts, obsessions and demons must be reabsorbed before Kia can become one with Chaos, and however useful they are in the short term the sorcerer must eventually recant."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step named is evocation in the reverse sense the work gives it here: what has been budded off by evocation has to be taken back. Liber AOM repeats the requirement for the adept, who reabsorbs sigils, gods, demons and the instruments of the sorcerer into himself."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicUnionOfKiaWithChaos ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Kia cannot become one with Chaos while one's thoughts, obsessions and demons remain unabsorbed." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Evocation, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Invocation: temporary possession by a god, communication from it, and manifestation of its powers"@en, "Invokation: zeitweilige Besessenheit durch einen Gott, Mitteilung von ihm und Offenbarwerden seiner Kräfte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician may need to rearrange or add to what he is: by seeking what he is not and transcending both in mutual annihilation, or by adding to his psyche any elements that are missing, a complete and balanced psychocosm being more easily surrendered. There are many maps of the mind, mostly inconsistent and fanciful; all mythology embodies a psychology and a complete pantheon resumes all of man's mental characteristics, so magicians often use a pagan pantheon to invoke a particular insight or ability, though anything from the archetypes of the collective unconscious to the elemental qualities of alchemy will serve. Figure 5 shows such maps: the seven planets, four elements, three alchemical elements, yin and yang, five tattwas, the sephiroth, eight trigrams and twelve astrological qualities. The aim of invocation is temporary possession by the god, communication from the god and manifestation of the god's magical powers, not the formation of religious cults."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated: the ultimate invocation, that of Kia, cannot be performed, since Kia has no dualised qualities by which to invoke it, and to give it one quality is to deny it another."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: if the magician taps a deep enough level of power, the god-forms may manifest with enough force to convince the mind of the god's objective existence. The work offers the conviction as a sign of depth, not as evidence of the god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, pp. 41-43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationMethod a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The method of invocation: total immersion in the god's qualities, then the life force united to its image by gnosis"@en, "Die Methode der Invokation: völliges Eintauchen in die Eigenschaften des Gottes, dann die Lebenskraft durch Gnosis mit seinem Bild vereint"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocation is a total immersion in the qualities of the desired form, invoking in every conceivable way. First the magician programmes himself into identity with the god by arranging all his experiences to coincide with its nature, in the most elaborate ritual surrounding himself with the sounds, smells, colours, instruments, memories, numbers, symbols, music and poetry suggestive of it. Second, he unites his life force to the god image with which he has united his mind, by techniques from the gnosis. In works of invocation, nothing is said to succeed like excess."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, pp. 41-44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationMethodYieldsInvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Immersion and gnosis are said to bring possession by the god and the god's powers"@en, "Eintauchen und Gnosis sollen Besessenheit durch den Gott und seine Kräfte bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Identity of mind with the god followed by union of the life force with its image is the work's method for the temporary possession, communication and manifestation of powers that invocation aims at."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationMethod ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, pp. 41-43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicArbitraryObsessions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing one's dominant obsessions and personality as arbitrary, and so more easily banished"@en, "Die eigenen vorherrschenden Besessenheiten und die Persönlichkeit als willkürlich erkennen und darum leichter bannen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shuffling of beliefs and desires that attends invocation is said to demonstrate that one's dominant obsessions or personality are quite arbitrary, and hence more easily banished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 41; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationYieldsArbitraryObsessions a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Shuffling beliefs in invocation is said to make one's dominant obsessions easier to banish"@en, "Das Mischen der Glaubenssätze in der Invokation soll die vorherrschenden Besessenheiten leichter bannbar machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical process of shuffling beliefs and desires in invocation demonstrates the arbitrariness of the personality and makes it more easily banished."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationMethod ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicArbitraryObsessions ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 41; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWarGodInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The invocation of the war god: crimson, steel, blood, drum and the war dance to Horus"@en, "Die Invokation des Kriegsgottes: Karmesin, Stahl, Blut, Trommel und der Kriegstanz für Horus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiate stands in a pentagonal chamber lit by five red lamps, robed in crimson with the skin of a bear or wolf, girded with steel weapons and crowned with iron; he has prepared his body by fasting, rigours, scourging and stimulants and turned his mind to things of Mars. He casts sulphur, oak and resins into the thurible, anoints himself with tiger balm, and opens the temple with a martial drum or a loud weapon fired into the air, having banished foreign influences, preferably by a pentagram ritual. Drawing blood from his right shoulder with a dagger he traces the sigil of Mars on his breast and the Eye of Horus on his brow, draws the symbols of Mars about him in crimson fire with a sword, and visualises himself as Horus. Then he dances wildly to his god, an assistant beating the rhythm, applying the scourge or firing guns, and chants a five-line invocation of Horus ending in the identity of god and dancer, until the god takes him into an ecstasy. Any of the props may be dispensed with by one whose Kia flows steadily into the willed artefacts of imagination."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the dance continues until the god takes the dancer into an ecstasy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, pp. 43-44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWarGodYieldsInvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The war dance is said to continue until the god takes the dancer into ecstasy"@en, "Der Kriegstanz soll andauern, bis der Gott den Tänzer in Ekstase nimmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The example rite ends when Horus takes the initiate into an ecstasy, which is the possession invocation aims at."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWarGodInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHorusNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Horus, invoked as the war god"@en, "Horus, als Kriegsgott angerufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Horus is the god of the example invocation of the war god: the initiate visualises himself in the form of the god Horus, traces the Eye of Horus on his brow and chants to him until the god takes him into ecstasy; the preparation and symbols are those of Mars."@en ;
    skos:note "Mars appears only as the planetary symbolism the rite uses, the sigil of Mars and things of Mars, and was not entered as a naming of its own."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Horus" ;
    pm:nameRole "war god" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationWithoutProps a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking without props, by Kia flowing steadily into the willed artefacts of imagination"@en, "Ohne Hilfsmittel invozieren, indem das Kia stetig in die gewollten Gebilde der Einbildung fließt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any of the props of the war-god rite can be dispensed with by anyone whose Kia flows steadily into the willed artefacts of imagination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicChaosBlackMass a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The latter-day black mass to the Great Mad Goddess Chaos, against the gods of logic"@en, "Die neuzeitliche schwarze Messe für die Große Verrückte Göttin Chaos, gegen die Götter der Logik"@de ;
    skos:definition "A blasphemy against the gods of logic and rationality, in which the Great Mad Goddess Chaos is invoked for her ecstasy and inspiration. Drumming, leaping and whirling in free movement are accompanied by idiotic incantations; forced deep breathing provokes hysterical laughter; mild hallucinogens and disinhibitory agents such as alcohol are taken with gasps of nitrous oxide; dice decide what unusual behaviour and sexual irregularities will take place; discordant music plays and flashing lights fall on clouds of incense; a great work of philosophy, preferably by Russell, burns open on the altar."@en ;
    skos:note "The work offers the rite as ideas for construction, not as a performed rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosEcstasyAndInspiration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ecstasy and inspiration of the Great Mad Goddess Chaos"@en, "Ekstase und Eingebung der Großen Verrückten Göttin Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Great Mad Goddess Chaos can be invoked for her ecstasy and inspiration; the overwhelming of the senses by a maelstrom of ingredients is the means."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackMassYieldsChaosEcstasy a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The black mass is said to invoke the goddess Chaos for her ecstasy and inspiration"@en, "Die schwarze Messe soll die Göttin Chaos um ihrer Ekstase und Eingebung willen anrufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A whole maelstrom of ingredients overcomes the senses, and the goddess is invoked for her ecstasy and inspiration."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChaosBlackMass ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicChaosEcstasyAndInspiration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGreatGoddessChaosNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Mad Goddess Chaos, who lends her power to her favourites"@en, "Die Große Verrückte Göttin Chaos, die ihren Lieblingen von ihrer Macht leiht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Great Mad Goddess Chaos is named as a lower aspect of the ultimate ground of existence in anthropomorphic form, invoked for her ecstasy and inspiration; in Liber NOX the universe is mad and arbitrary in her ways, and the Great Goddess Chaos lends some of her power to those who can become her favourites."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Great Mad Goddess Chaos" ;
    pm:nameRole "goddess" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, p. 59; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSaturnInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The invocation of Saturn: lead-wrapped in near darkness, a mock burial and the corpse arising"@en, "Die Invokation des Saturn: in Blei gewickelt im Beinahe-Dunkel, ein Scheinbegräbnis und der aufsteigende Leichnam"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiate prepares by fasting, sleeplessness and exhaustion and retires to a chamber in near total darkness lit only by three sticks of a resinous, cloying, musty incense. He weighs his body down with sheets of lead wrapped round limbs, trunk and head, otherwise cold and naked, and to a slow, monotonous drumbeat conducts a mock burial of himself; with extreme caution he may take small quantities of atropine-like solanum alkaloids. Then he meditates on himself as a corpse or skeleton rising slowly from the tomb in a tattered winding sheet and taking up the scythe of office."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSaturnYieldsInvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The mock burial is the work's way of invoking Saturn, god of death"@en, "Das Scheinbegräbnis ist der Weg des Werkes, Saturn, den Gott des Todes, anzurufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rite is offered as the manner in which Saturn might be invoked, ending in the meditation on oneself as the corpse in the aspect of the god."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSaturnInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSaturnNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Saturn, the God of Death"@en, "Saturn, der Gott des Todes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Saturn is named as the God of Death, invoked by fasting, darkness, lead and a mock burial, the initiate meditating on himself as a corpse that rises and takes up the scythe of office."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Saturn" ;
    pm:nameRole "god of death" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSolanumCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Solanum alkaloids only in small quantities and with extreme caution"@en, "Solanum-Alkaloide nur in kleinen Mengen und mit äußerster Vorsicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the invocation of Saturn small quantities of atropine-like solanum alkaloids may be taken, and only with extreme caution."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicSaturnInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Invocation, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLiberatingBehaviour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liberating behaviour: what widens one's options, and choice of duality instead of freedom from it"@en, "Befreiendes Verhalten: was die eigenen Möglichkeiten erweitert, und Wahl der Dualität statt Freiheit von ihr"@de ;
    skos:definition "In creating life Chaos has always sought to increase its possibilities of expression, and the most flexible, adaptable, inclusive and complex creatures, cultures, men and ideas always win out; to seek these qualities is said to give more liberation than renunciation or political reorganisation. No belief is more liberated than another; the possibility of change is what matters, and every new liberation is destined to become another enslavement for most of its adherents. There is no freedom from duality on this plane, only choice of duality. Liberating behaviour increases one's possibilities for future action and limiting behaviour narrows them; the secret of freedom is not to be drawn into situations where one's alternatives become few or one. The chief techniques are those that weaken the hold of society, convention and habit and lead to a more expansive outlook: sacrilege, heresy, iconoclasm, bioaestheticism and anathemism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, pp. 45-47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOmnivorousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming omnivorous: able to think, believe or do half a dozen different things"@en, "Allesfressend werden: fähig, ein halbes Dutzend verschiedener Dinge zu denken, zu glauben oder zu tun"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since casting off one set of chains means adopting another, unless one lives in a reduced and impoverished style that is itself a limitation, the solution is to become omnivorous: someone who can think, believe or do any of half a dozen different things is called more free than someone confined to one. Sufi mystics are said for this reason to have been required to master a handful of secular trades besides their occult studies."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, p. 45; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Liberation is an abominably difficult path that means leaving one's culture and personality"@en, "Die Befreiung ist ein abscheulich schwerer Weg, der das Verlassen der eigenen Kultur und Persönlichkeit bedeutet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The path is called abominably difficult: it means stepping outside one's culture, society, relationships, family, personality, beliefs, prejudices, opinions and ideas, the very chains that give most people definition, meaning, character and belonging; and in casting off one set one cannot avoid adopting another."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicLiberatingBehaviour ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, p. 45; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationFromConditioning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liberation from the hold of society, convention and habit"@en, "Befreiung vom Griff der Gesellschaft, der Konvention und der Gewohnheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state the five techniques of liberation aim at: the hold of society, convention and habit over the initiate weakened, and a more expansive outlook gained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, pp. 45-47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSacrilege a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sacrilege: a glorious act of disobedience or blasphemy against one's conditioning"@en, "Sakrileg: ein herrlicher Akt des Ungehorsams oder der Lästerung gegen die eigene Prägung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The individual breaks through rules of conditioning with some glorious act of disobedience or blasphemy: put a brick through your television, explore sexualities unusual to you, do something you normally feel to be utterly revolting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Sacrilege, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSacrilegeYieldsLiberation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sacrilege is said to liberate energy that strengthens the spirit and gives courage"@en, "Das Sakrileg soll Energie freisetzen, die den Geist stärkt und Mut gibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Energy is said to be liberated when conditioning is broken by disobedience or blasphemy, strengthening the spirit and giving courage for further insurrection."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSacrilege ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationFromConditioning ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Sacrilege, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFutureFreedomCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Anything is permitted that does not restrict one's own or another's future freedom of action"@en, "Erlaubt ist alles, was die künftige Handlungsfreiheit von einem selbst oder anderen nicht einschränkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "You are free to do anything, however extreme, so long as it will not restrict your own or somebody else's future freedom of action."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicSacrilege ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Sacrilege, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicHeresy a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Heresy: seeking out bizarre and suppressed ideas, and always disagreeing in argument"@en, "Häresie: bizarre und unterdrückte Ideen aufsuchen und im Streit immer widersprechen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiate seeks out ideas that seem bizarre, crazy, extreme, arbitrary, contradictory and nonsensical; whatever is suppressed, ridiculed or despised almost always holds a telling counterpoint to mainstream ideas. In argument he always disagrees, especially if the opponent begins to voice his own opinions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Heresy, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHeresyYieldsLiberation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Heresy is said to show that one's reasonable ideas are just as bizarre as the others"@en, "Die Häresie soll zeigen, dass die eigenen vernünftigen Ideen ebenso bizarr sind wie die anderen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By seeking out bizarre ideas one finds that the ideas one clung to as reasonable, sensible and humanitarian are just as bizarre and arbitrary."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicHeresy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationFromConditioning ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Heresy, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicIconoclasm a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Iconoclasm: contemplating the gulfs between society's theory and its practice"@en, "Ikonoklasmus: die Klüfte zwischen Theorie und Praxis der Gesellschaft betrachten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initiate contrasts pornography and romance, gluttony and famine, dignity and masturbation; considers violence as entertainment and mass slaughter for ideals; looks at what is done in the name of religion and of the consumer society; and extends the list indefinitely, picking through society's dirty underwear to discover its real habits. Cynicism, sadness or laughter is called the magician's privilege."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Iconoclasm, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIconoclasmYieldsLiberation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Iconoclasm is offered among the techniques that weaken the hold of society"@en, "Der Ikonoklasmus wird unter den Techniken angeboten, die den Griff der Gesellschaft lockern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Breaking society's images by exposing its real habits is one of the five techniques the work names for weakening the hold of society and convention."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicIconoclasm ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationFromConditioning ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Iconoclasm, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBioaestheticism a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Bioaestheticism: trusting the body and satisfying its needs of food, warmth, sex and transcendence"@en, "Bioästhetizismus: dem Körper vertrauen und seine Bedürfnisse nach Nahrung, Wärme, Sexualität und Transzendenz stillen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The body is called more trustworthy than all the sages and wiser than a great library; it asks only for food, warmth, sex and transcendence, the urge to become one with something greater, satisfied in love, humanitarian works, or artistic, scientific or magical quests. Power, authority, excessive wealth and greed for sensory experience are aberrations of these needs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Bioaestheticism, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBioaestheticismYieldsLiberation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "To satisfy the body's simple needs is called liberation indeed"@en, "Die einfachen Bedürfnisse des Körpers zu stillen heißt Befreiung"@de ;
    skos:definition "To satisfy the simple needs of food, warmth, sex and transcendence is said to be liberation indeed."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBioaestheticism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLiberationFromConditioning ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Bioaestheticism, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAnathemism a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Anathemism: eating the loathsome, scheming against one's sacred principles, cutting down every belief"@en, "Anathemismus: das Ekelhafte essen, gegen die eigenen heiligen Grundsätze planen, jede Überzeugung niederhauen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A technique practised directly on oneself. Eat all loathsome things till they no longer revolt; seek union with all that you normally reject; scheme against your most sacred principles in thought, word and deed; reflect on the transitory and contingent nature of all things, since you will witness the loss or putrefaction of every loved thing; examine every belief, preference and opinion and cut it down. The intellect is called a sword whose use is to prevent identification with any phenomenon, and the most powerful minds cling to the fewest fixed principles."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Anathemism, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDiscardingPersonality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Possessing one's personality: being able to manipulate or discard it at will"@en, "Die eigene Persönlichkeit besitzen: sie nach Willen handhaben oder ablegen können"@de ;
    skos:definition "An idea is not completely understood until one understands the conditions under which it is not true, and likewise one cannot be said to possess a personality until one can manipulate or discard it at will. The personality is a mask of convenience that sticks to the face; the only clear view is said to be from atop the mountain of one's dead selves."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Anathemism, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAnathemismYieldsDiscardingPersonality a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Anathemism is said to free one from the identifications that make up the ego"@en, "Der Anathemismus soll von den Identifikationen befreien, aus denen das Ich besteht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sidestepping convention still leaves the prejudices and preferences that give the ego definition; anathemism, practised on oneself, cuts them down until the personality can be manipulated or discarded at will."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAnathemism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDiscardingPersonality ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Liberation, Anathemism, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAugoeidesNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Augoeides, Genius or Holy Guardian Angel, the avatar of Kia on earth"@en, "Der Augoeides, Genius oder Heilige Schutzengel, der Avatar des Kia auf Erden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician's genius, daemon, true will or Augoeides is defined as the most perfect vehicle of Kia on the plane of duality, its avatar on earth, representing the magician's true will and his purpose in existing; its invocation is traditionally the Knowledge and Conversation of the Holy Guardian Angel. It is not something added to oneself but revealed by stripping away excess, the god within."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Augoeides" ;
    pm:nameRole "genius, Holy Guardian Angel" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, pp. 49-50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKnowledgeAndConversation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Knowledge and Conversation of the Holy Guardian Angel: discovery of one's true will"@en, "Die Kenntnis und das Gespräch des Heiligen Schutzengels: Entdeckung des eigenen wahren Willens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician's most important invocation, also called the Magnum Opus or Great Work: the discovery of one's true will or real nature, a progressive metamorphosis and complete overhaul of one's existence whose blueprint is sought on the way. Kia is said to have incarnated in these conditions for a purpose, each incarnation being a task or puzzle; in a completely chaotic universe there are no accidents, and the key to the puzzle lies in the phenomena of one's own plane, as in a labyrinth whose turning walls must be watched. The operation is usually a lengthy one."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: as the magician manifests more of his true will, the Augoeides is said to reveal images, names and spiritual principles by which it can be drawn into greater manifestation; the spoken invocation, halting at first, is said to prove itself in time. Neither is a sign another person could see."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, pp. 49-50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Metamorphosis, p. 16; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMomentumOfTheUniverse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The momentum of the universe and amazing good luck for one doing his true will"@en, "Der Schwung des Universums und erstaunliches Glück für den, der seinen wahren Willen tut"@de ;
    skos:definition "A person doing his true will is said to be assisted by the momentum of the universe and to seem possessed of amazing good luck; the chapter on enchantment closes on the same sentence."@en ;
    skos:note "The good luck is the only sign the work gives that one is doing one's true will, and it gives no sign that one is not; the next pages require the magician to read every misfortune as a reflection of how he conducts his existence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalWorldView a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The vow to interpret every manifestation of existence as a message from Chaos to oneself"@en, "Das Gelübde, jede Erscheinung des Daseins als Botschaft des Chaos an einen selbst zu deuten"@de ;
    skos:definition "In beginning the work of the knowledge and conversation the magician vows to interpret every manifestation of existence as a direct message from the infinite Chaos to himself personally. He takes complete responsibility for his present incarnation and considers every experience, thing or piece of information that reaches him from any source as a reflection of the way he conducts his existence; the idea that things happen to one unrelated to one's actions is called an illusion of shallow awareness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, pp. 49-50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The daily invocation of the Augoeides at dawn, drawn into oneself and never closed by banishing"@en, "Die tägliche Invokation des Augoeides bei Tagesanbruch, in sich hineingenommen und nie durch Bannung beschlossen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Directly on waking, preferably at dawn, the initiate goes to the place of invocation, thinking that each day's new birth brings the chance of greater rebirth. He banishes the temple of his mind by ritual or magical trance; unveils a token, symbol or sigil that represents the Holy Guardian Angel to him, likely to change as inspiration moves him; invokes an image of the Angel into his mind's eye, as a luminous double of his own form before or behind him or a ball of brilliant light above his head; and formulates his aspirations, humbling himself in prayer or exalting himself in loud proclamation, best spontaneously from the heart. He aims to establish ideas and images corresponding to his genius and to receive inspiration from it. Having communicated with the invoked form he draws it into himself and goes forth to live as he has willed. The rite may close with an aspiration to the wisdom of silence by brief concentration on the sigil of the Augoeides, never by banishing; at times more elaborate rituals with stronger gnosis may be used. At the end of the day an accounting and fresh resolution are made, and though every day be a catalogue of failure there is to be no sense of sin or guilt, which are symptoms of imbalance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocationYieldsKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The daily invocation is said to draw the genius into greater manifestation"@en, "Die tägliche Invokation soll den Genius in größere Offenbarung ziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "As the magician manifests more of his true will through the daily work, the Augoeides is said to reveal images, names and principles by which it can be drawn into greater manifestation; the invocation is the operation of obtaining the knowledge and conversation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicKnowledgeAndConversation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWorldViewBeforeInvocation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Keeping watch on the walls of the labyrinth comes before the invocation of the genius"@en, "Die Wache über die Wände des Labyrinths geht der Invokation des Genius voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Keeping a close eye on the walls of the labyrinth, the conditions of his existence, the magician may then begin his invocation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalWorldView ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNoBanishingCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The invocation of the Augoeides is never concluded by banishing"@en, "Die Invokation des Augoeides wird nie durch Bannung beschlossen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ritual may be concluded by a brief concentration on the sigil of the Augoeides, but never by banishing."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGuiltCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "No sin or guilt, though every day be a catalogue of failure"@en, "Keine Sünde und keine Schuld, auch wenn jeder Tag ein Verzeichnis des Scheiterns ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Though every day be a catalogue of failure, there should be no sense of sin or guilt: magic is the raising of the whole individual in perfect balance to the power of infinity, and such feelings are symptoms of imbalance."@en ;
    skos:note "The daily accounting is the one place where the work expects failure as the normal entry; it is to be recorded and not felt."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAugoeidesInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChoronzonCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "False identification with the genius leads to obsession, madness and the demon Choronzon"@en, "Falsche Identifikation mit dem Genius führt zu Besessenheit, Wahnsinn und zum Dämon Choronzon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The discovery of one's true will is called difficult and dangerous, since a false identification leads to obsession and madness. If unnecessary or imbalanced scraps of ego become identified with the genius by mistake, disaster awaits: the life force flows into these complexes and bloats them into grotesque monsters known as the demon Choronzon, and some magicians going too fast with the invocation have failed to banish this demon and have gone spectacularly insane."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with an outcome: going too fast with the invocation and failing to banish Choronzon ends in spectacular insanity."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicKnowledgeAndConversation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 51; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChoronzonNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Choronzon, the demon of ego complexes mistaken for the genius"@en, "Choronzon, der Dämon der mit dem Genius verwechselten Ich-Komplexe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Choronzon is named as the demon into which the life force bloats imbalanced scraps of ego identified by mistake with the genius; magicians who went too fast and failed to banish it went spectacularly insane."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Choronzon" ;
    pm:nameRole "demon" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Augoeides, p. 51; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRipples a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination as intercepting the ripples every event sends through space and time"@en, "Divination als Auffangen der Wellen, die jedes Ereignis durch Raum und Zeit schickt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Space, time, mass and energy originate from Chaos and are moved by it through the aether; some densities of the aether are only partly differentiated and indeterminate in space and time. As mass curves space-time and extends to infinity as gravity, so all events, especially those involving the mind, send ripples through all creation, and the mantic art is the intercepting and interpreting of them. The ripples can be received only if they strike a note of resonance in the receiver and are not drowned by noise or suppressed by the psychic censor; resonance exists naturally between mother and child or between lovers, and otherwise is established by concentrating on the object of divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination: answering a question by magical perception, directly or through symbols"@en, "Divination: eine Frage durch magische Wahrnehmung beantworten, unmittelbar oder durch Symbole"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mantic art: intercepting and interpreting the ripples of events by silencing the mind and letting impressions arise, or, where direct prescience is not possible, by symbolic intermediaries that carry the answer into the conscious mind. Charged instruments, magical mirrors, crystal spheres, polished surfaces and pools of dark liquid or blood, are said to hold formless aetheric energy that amplifies the impressions, where most tools only receive them."@en ;
    skos:note "Astrology is called no valid form of magical divination: it assumes a causal relation between events linked only weakly if at all, owes its successes to the natural prescience of its practitioners and obscures its failures by imprecision, evasiveness and ambiguity. Entered at the claim and not as a dispute, since no astrological work is cited. The same charge, failure hidden by imprecision, is the one a test would bring against divination in this work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, pp. 52-53; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Magic, p. 19; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPrescience a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Direct prescience, the ideal of divination"@en, "Unmittelbares Vorauswissen, das Ideal der Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "Direct prescience, knowing without symbolic intermediary, is called the ideal of divination and, like the creation of direct effects in enchantment, a high point of the art; it is not always possible. For the adept it becomes, with direct enchantment, one of his only tools."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTranceDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination in trance: the matter concentrated on intensely, then impressions let arise into the void"@en, "Divination in der Trance: der Gegenstand stark konzentriert, dann Eindrücke in die Leere aufsteigen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The general level of mental noise is suppressed by silencing the mind by some gnostic method, usually of the inhibitory mode, which also aids concentration. Any form of magical trance can be adapted by first directing intense concentration on the matter of divination, or a sigilised form of it, and then allowing impressions to arise into the vacuous state of consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTranceDivinationYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Concentration followed by a vacuous mind is said to let the divinatory impression arise"@en, "Konzentration, gefolgt von leerem Geist, soll den divinatorischen Eindruck aufsteigen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Concentrating on the matter establishes the resonance, silencing the mind suppresses the noise, and the impression arises into the vacuous consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTranceDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicResonanceBeforeDivination a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The ripples are received only where there is resonance, and otherwise it is made by concentration"@en, "Die Wellen werden nur bei Resonanz empfangen, und sonst wird sie durch Konzentration hergestellt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ripples through space and time can only be received if they strike a note of resonance in the receiver and are not drowned out by noise or suppressed by the psychic censor; where no natural resonance exists it has to be established by concentrating on the object of divination."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work names three ways the reception fails, no resonance, noise, and the censor."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicDivination ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicTranceDivination ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without resonance, or with too much noise or the censor's suppression, the ripples are not received." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEroticComatoseLucidity a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Eroto-comatose lucidity: sexuality stimulated and exhausted until the borderland of consciousness"@en, "Eroto-komatöse Klarheit: Sexualität erregt und erschöpft bis an die Grenze des Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "A condition brought about by continually stimulating and exhausting the sexuality by any possible means until the mind enters the borderland state between consciousness and unconsciousness. Liber NOX gives it as a variant of autoerotic congress: the sexuality is stimulated again and again until consciousness slips into the shadow world through which subconscious images and divinatory impressions flow; the body should be neither too tired nor too comfortable, so that deep sleep does not supervene, and karezza, excitation that repeatedly approaches orgasm without reaching it, may be used, since the body, particularly the male, may not sustain many orgasms."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: a body too tired or too comfortable falls into the unconsciousness of deep sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 70-71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEroticLucidityYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Eroto-comatose lucidity is said to open the borderland through which divinatory impressions flow"@en, "Eroto-komatöse Klarheit soll das Grenzland öffnen, durch das divinatorische Eindrücke fließen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Of the excitatory techniques, sex is called the easiest for divination; the borderland it reaches is where subconscious images and divinatory impressions flow."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEroticComatoseLucidity ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 52; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 70-71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSymbolicDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination by symbols: drawn below the threshold of deliberateness but above pure randomness"@en, "Divination durch Symbole: gezogen unterhalb der Schwelle der Absicht, aber oberhalb des reinen Zufalls"@de ;
    skos:definition "Assuming magical perception can forge a tenuous connection with the answer, symbols are shuffled, drawn or selected to carry it into the conscious mind, and a further effort of interpretation brings the perception into full manifestation. Any system can serve; the difficulty is forging the magic link. The magician lets the magic slip in below conscious control without letting the process become merely random: in cartomancy one looks through the pack first and then shuffles only lightly. The symbol is then used as a basis for lateral thinking, intuitive guesswork, rather than as a final answer mechanically interpreted. The best methods lie just below the threshold of deliberateness and above that of randomness: shamanistic casting of bones, stones or rune-marked sticks is called simplest and best."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with its mechanism: shuffling too thoroughly makes the result completely random and lowers the spread's power to stimulate magical perception; symbolic intermediaries can augment divination greatly or ruin it utterly. The work adds that coins, dice and yarrow stalks grew more complex as prescient ability faded, and that highly complex mathematical systems are the decadence of the art."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, pp. 52-53; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSymbolicDivinationYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Symbols drawn lightly are said to carry the answer into the conscious mind"@en, "Leicht gezogene Symbole sollen die Antwort ins Bewusstsein tragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Symbols selected just below deliberateness carry the answer forged by magical perception into the conscious mind, where interpretation crystallises it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSymbolicDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 53; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicCensor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The psychic censor, which hides dreams, protects from sensory flood, and obstructs divination"@en, "Der psychische Zensor, der Träume verbirgt, vor der Reizflut schützt und die Divination hemmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Of all the forces that obstruct divination none is said to have more power over civilised consciousness than the psychic censor, the same factor that denies us most of our dream experiences and keeps us from being overwhelmed by the millions of sensory impressions that bombard the body. We could not function without it, but it is useful to be able to turn parts of it off at times."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, pp. 53-54; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber MMM, Dreaming, p. 23; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHallucinogenCensorCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Hallucinogens knock out the psychic censor unselectively and are not much use"@en, "Halluzinogene schalten den psychischen Zensor wahllos aus und taugen wenig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hallucinogenic drugs knock the psychic censor out unselectively and are not much use."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated with its reason: the drug removes the censor without selection."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicCensor ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 54; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNotingCoincidences a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Noting every coincidence and foreknowing instead of dismissing it, and recording it in the diary"@en, "Jeden Zufall und jedes Vorwissen bemerken, statt es abzutun, und es im Tagebuch festhalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician begins to notice all the coincidences around him instead of dismissing them. Often one knew that something would be said or happen just before it did, several times a day, and yet dismisses it each time; a definite effort is made to note these occurrences consciously and to record them in the diary."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: once noted and recorded, the coincidences are said to become much more numerous, so many that the word coincidence becomes ridiculous, and one is becoming prescient. The sign is produced by the act of noting; the work gives no count of misses to set against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 54; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNotingCoincidencesYieldsPrescience a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Noting and recording coincidences is said to make one prescient"@en, "Das Bemerken und Aufzeichnen von Zufällen soll vorauswissend machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If a definite effort is made to note and record them, the occurrences become much more numerous, and one is becoming prescient."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicNotingCoincidences ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPrescience ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Divination, p. 54; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment: making things happen by magical will, directly or through symbols and sigils"@en, "Verzauberung: Dinge durch magischen Willen geschehen lassen, unmittelbar oder durch Symbole und Sigillen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical will may exert its effects directly on the universe or use symbols or sigils as intermediaries; making things happen by either method is the casting of enchantments, the creation of direct effects being a high point of the art and as elusive as prescience. From a magical point of view it is axiomatic that we have created the world in which we exist: the magician may say that thus does his Kia manifest. Things go on existing when unobserved because they have their being in Chaos, and the magician can change something only by matching the Chaos that upholds the normal event, his will becoming the will of the universe in that aspect. Such magic works much better with several people in perfect synchronisation, and is even harder before many persons who all uphold the ordinary course of events."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated: direct enchantment is harder to perform in front of many persons who uphold the ordinary course of events. It is a condition that would also explain in advance why a magic observed by many fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWillAsUnityOfDesire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Will as unity of desire, not will-power"@en, "Wille als Einheit des Begehrens, nicht Willenskraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Will is not will-power, virility, obstinacy or hardness; will is unity of desire. It expresses itself best against no resistance, when its action passes unnoticed, and only when the mind is in a state of multiple desire do we see the agonies of will-power. The will always manifests as the victory of the strongest desire, and as reorganisation of belief is the key to liberation, so reorganisation of desire is the key to will."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with its reason: pitting oneself against oaths, abstentions and tests is said merely to set up conflicts in the mind, and the ego reacts with disgust when its chosen desire fails. The work rejects here the discipline other works of the corpus prescribe; the oath of The Book of Results is its nearest counterpart (pmp:ChaosMagicOath in the same file)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, pp. 55-56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWillChauvinismCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The most fatal pitfall: confusing will with the chauvinism of the ego"@en, "Die verhängnisvollste Falle: den Willen mit dem Chauvinismus des Ich verwechseln"@de ;
    skos:definition "In trying to develop the will, the most fatal pitfall is said to be confusing will with the chauvinism of the ego."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicWillAsUnityOfDesire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWitnessNauseaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Those who witness real magic at close range may be overcome with nausea and may die"@en, "Wer echte Magie aus nächster Nähe bezeugt, kann von Übelkeit überwältigt werden und sterben"@de ;
    skos:definition "People who witness real magical happenings at close range are said sometimes to be overcome with nausea and even to die, because the part of their life force that upheld the normal reality is forcibly altered when the abnormal occurs."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUnityBeforeAct a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Desires are rearranged into unity before an act, not during it"@en, "Die Begierden werden vor einer Handlung zur Einheit geordnet, nicht während ihrer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician seeks unity of desire before he attempts to act; desires are rearranged before an act, not during it, and in all things he must live so."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicWillAsUnityOfDesire ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMatchingChaosBeforeChange a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The magician can change an event only if he matches the Chaos upholding it"@en, "Der Magier kann ein Ereignis nur ändern, wenn er dem Chaos entspricht, das es trägt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician can only change something if he can match the Chaos that is upholding the normal event, which is the same as becoming one with the source of the event."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicUnionOfKiaWithChaos ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without matching the Chaos that upholds the event, the magician cannot change it." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEnchantmentBySigil a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment by sigil, wax image or magical alphabet, cast in gnostic exaltation while events are embryonic"@en, "Verzauberung durch Sigille, Wachsbild oder magisches Alphabet, in gnostischer Erhebung geworfen, solange die Ereignisse im Keim sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Many difficulties are got round by sigils: the desire is represented by a pictorial glyph, by a wax image to be wounded, bound or healed, by the characters of a magical alphabet, or by an image in the mind's eye, each serving as a focus for the will; concentration on the spell is augmented by gnostic exaltation to cast the enchantment. It is called infinitely easier to manipulate events while they are still embryonic or at their inception, so turning causality to advantage rather than opposing it."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the desire then manifests as a convenient but strange coincidence rather than as a startling discontinuity. A coincidence is also the outcome a sceptic would predict; the work names the overlap and counts it as success."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentBySigilYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The sigil cast in gnosis is said to make the desire manifest as a strange coincidence"@en, "Die in der Gnosis geworfene Sigille soll das Begehren als seltsamen Zufall eintreten lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Focused on a sigil and cast with gnostic exaltation while events are embryonic, the desire is said to manifest as a convenient but strange coincidence."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEnchantmentBySigil ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicObservingLuck a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Observing the current of one's luck in small matters and extending it"@en, "Den Strom des eigenen Glücks in kleinen Dingen beobachten und ausdehnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician observes the current of his luck in small, inconsequential matters, finds the conditions for its success, and tries to extend his luck in various small ways."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStrengthenedWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The will strengthened, by the concentrations of trance or by luck"@en, "Der gestärkte Wille, durch die Konzentrationen der Trance oder durch das Glück"@de ;
    skos:definition "The will may be strengthened by one technique besides the concentrations of magical trance, and that is by luck."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLuckYieldsStrengthenedWill a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Working with luck is said to strengthen the will"@en, "Die Arbeit mit dem Glück soll den Willen stärken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Aside from the concentrations of magical trance, the will may be strengthened by observing and extending one's luck."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicObservingLuck ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicStrengthenedWill ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber LUX, Enchantment, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritOfBlackMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The spirit of black magic: existence as the great indulgence, and the Self as God"@en, "Der Geist der schwarzen Magie: das Dasein als die große Hingabe, und das Selbst als Gott"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical power is called the key to the heaven-hell of the now; lacking it, many slip into the greyness of small fears and desires and invent hereafters. The life force seeks the flesh through endless incarnations, since without it Kia has no mirror and there is no awareness; the condition of the flesh is dual, and eternal warfare is the price, purpose and reward of existence. Existence is the great indulgence, and any attempt to avoid part of oneself invites loss of form. The Self alone is God and should recognise itself in all things; acceptance and living without restraint are called the highest virtue, and the greatest sinners the greatest saints. The I is multiple, a colony of beings in one envelope bound by Kia's self-love. A god is man wielding the force of Chaos: to him nothing is true and everything is permitted, he chooses his own purpose, has bound himself to earth forever and reincarnates at will. Liber NOX is laid out by its schema, figure 6, through sorcery, the double, transmogrification, ecstasy, random belief and the Alphabet of Desire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, pp. 57-59; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSmallFearsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Opposing the great dualities with lesser fears and desires is the beginning of failure"@en, "Den großen Dualitäten mit kleineren Ängsten und Wünschen entgegenzutreten ist der Anfang des Scheiterns"@de ;
    skos:definition "To oppose the great dualities, sex and death, pain and pleasure, with lesser fears and desires is called the beginning of failure of satisfaction; any attempt to avoid part of oneself invites loss of form, a self-negation leading to a shrinkage of spirit, and those who uphold limited values bind themselves to mediocrity and failure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritOfBlackMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, p. 59; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicReservingKia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Reserving Kia for inspiration, ecstasy and magic: plain food, revulsion and delight tasted only occasionally"@en, "Das Kia für Eingebung, Ekstase und Magie aufsparen: einfache Nahrung, Abscheu und Wonne nur gelegentlich gekostet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The gourmet's small pleasures are said to have come full circle toward excrement with little satisfaction; the author would rather take plain wholesome food for the body and taste the nectars of revulsion and delight only occasionally, reserving Kia for works of inspiration, ecstasy and magic. If an emotion is pushed to the furthest realms of non-necessitation, nothing is left to balance ecstasy but ecstasy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, pp. 59-60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSeekingPleasureCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Seeking pleasure is the surest invocation of displeasure"@en, "Das Suchen der Lust ist die sicherste Anrufung der Unlust"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seeking pleasure is called the surest invocation of displeasure, by which one falls back rapidly into the general greyness. The supplementary alphabet says the same of hedonism, which inevitably leads to an epicureanism of pain, ever more poisonous and debilitating pleasures provoking a reaction from exhausted senses, until hedonism and masochism exhaust themselves into the numbed greyness of dulled faculties."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicReservingKia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, p. 60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 86; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorcery a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sorcery: material bases that build up power over time, in four kinds"@en, "Hexerei: stoffliche Grundlagen, die über die Zeit Kraft ansammeln, in vier Arten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sorcery is the art of using material bases to effect magical transformations, the advantage being that the power residing in them can be built up over a period. Four types are used: those holding reserves of a particular power, fetishes, talismans, spirit traps and amulets; those carrying an effect to its target, powders, philtres, wax images and knotted cords; those serving as a basis to receive divinatory impressions; and those anchoring an aetheric form that can be sent like a weapon. Blood and semen may be used as sources of life force, and hair, fingernails, spittle and other secreta give magical links with target persons."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTalismanCharging a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Charging a talisman with a simple charge, its form suggestive of the effect"@en, "Ein Talisman mit einer einfachen Ladung laden, seine Form der Wirkung entsprechend"@de ;
    skos:definition "Talismans, amulets and fetishes are charged by a process analogous to evocation. A talisman usually receives some simple charge; the form and composition of the material base should be suggestive of the desired effect."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: if the talisman is well enough made it should continue to evoke its effect even when not being used for concentration."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTalismanicStates a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "States evoked by a talisman: strength, courage, health, virility, no-mind, sleep"@en, "Von einem Talisman hervorgerufene Zustände: Stärke, Mut, Gesundheit, Manneskraft, Nicht-Geist, Schlaf"@de ;
    skos:definition "A charged talisman is said to evoke in its possessor, when he concentrates on it, strength, courage, health, virility, no-mind, sleep, or some other emotion or state of power, each named singly by the work as an example of the simple charge."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTalismanYieldsStates a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A charged talisman is said to evoke its state in the possessor who concentrates on it"@en, "Ein geladener Talisman soll in seinem Besitzer, der sich auf ihn sammelt, seinen Zustand hervorrufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The talisman evokes its charge when concentrated on, and if well made even when not."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTalismanCharging ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTalismanicStates ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAmuletMaking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Making an amulet: a small figure into which an aetheric duplicate is placed by the strongest evocation"@en, "Ein Amulett machen: eine kleine Figur, in die durch stärkste Evokation ein ätherisches Doppel gelegt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Amulets are objects containing a portion of aetheric and life force with a particular task, and are semi-sentient. They are fashioned as a small man or creature, and during the evocation an aetheric duplicate is placed inside the material form. When made by a group or tribe over a long period they are called fetishes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAmuletProtection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Protection of places or persons within a short radius by an amulet"@en, "Schutz von Orten oder Personen in kurzem Umkreis durch ein Amulett"@de ;
    skos:definition "Amulets are said to be most commonly made to protect places or persons within a short radius of action."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAmuletYieldsProtection a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The amulet is said to protect places or persons within its short radius"@en, "Das Amulett soll Orte oder Personen in seinem kurzen Umkreis schützen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The semi-sentient amulet performs its task, most commonly protection within a short radius."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAmuletMaking ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAmuletProtection ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStrongestEvocationForAmulets a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Amulets can be created only by the strongest forms of evocation"@en, "Amulette lassen sich nur durch die stärksten Formen der Evokation erschaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Amulets, being semi-sentient, can only be created by the strongest forms of evocation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAmuletMaking ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicEvocation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the strongest forms of evocation no amulet can be created." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCrystalSpiritTrap a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Quartz crystals as spirit traps: left near charged things, or plunged into a spirit's form"@en, "Quarzkristalle als Geisterfallen: bei geladenen Dingen gelassen oder in die Gestalt eines Geistes gestoßen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any material base is in some way a spirit trap, but crystals absorb aetheric imprints very readily. Quartz crystals can be left near charged places, persons or objects to pick up impressions; a spirit or elemental found inhabiting a place can be trapped by plunging a crystal into its form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritTrapping a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Picking up aetheric impressions in a crystal, and trapping a spirit in it"@en, "Ätherische Eindrücke in einem Kristall auffangen und einen Geist darin fangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two effects of the crystal named at one place: picking up the impressions of charged places, persons or objects, and trapping a spirit or elemental that inhabits a place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCrystalYieldsSpiritTrapping a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A quartz crystal is said to pick up impressions and to trap a spirit plunged into"@en, "Ein Quarzkristall soll Eindrücke aufnehmen und einen Geist fangen, in den er gestoßen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Crystals absorb aetheric imprints very readily, so they pick up impressions and can trap a spirit."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCrystalSpiritTrap ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritTrapping ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEnchantmentBySorcery a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment by sorcery: powders, philtres, wax images and knotted cords imprinted with desire and placed by the victim"@en, "Verzauberung durch Hexerei: Pulver, Tränke, Wachsbilder und Knotenschnüre, mit Begehren geprägt und dem Opfer in den Weg gelegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The material base, composed of anything suggestive of the result and perhaps including possessions or parts of the body of the intended victim, is imprinted with the magician's desire by concentration, visualisation and gnostic exaltation while it is compounded; the charged matter is then placed where the victim will come into contact with it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The charged material base is said to carry the enchantment to its victim"@en, "Die geladene stoffliche Grundlage soll die Verzauberung zu ihrem Opfer tragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Material bases of the carrying kind act to carry an effect to its target once the victim comes into contact with them."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEnchantmentBySorcery ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, pp. 61-62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorOfDarkness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The mirror of darkness: black glass or obsidian, charged by gazing in no-mind until it opens like a tunnel"@en, "Der Spiegel der Finsternis: schwarzes Glas oder Obsidian, durch Schauen im Nicht-Geist geladen, bis er sich wie ein Tunnel öffnet"@de ;
    skos:definition "An instrument of black glass or natural obsidian, carried concealed close to the body. It is charged by using it as the focus of the magical trance of no-mindedness, gazing into it unwaveringly for long periods until it opens like a pit or tunnel beneath one. Liber NOX also calls the Sigil of Chaos a mirror of darkness for communication between the order's adepts."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the mirror opens like a pit or tunnel beneath the gazer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFarSeeing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Perception reaching through the mirror's tunnel to other regions of time and space"@en, "Wahrnehmung, die durch den Tunnel des Spiegels in andere Bereiche von Zeit und Raum reicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once the aetheric tunnel has developed, perception is said to reach through it as the will directs to other regions of time and space."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMirrorYieldsFarSeeing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The opened mirror of darkness is said to let perception reach other regions of time and space"@en, "Der geöffnete Spiegel der Finsternis soll die Wahrnehmung andere Bereiche von Zeit und Raum erreichen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The perception reaches through the tunnel as the will directs."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorOfDarkness ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFarSeeing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTunnelBeforeUse a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The mirror of darkness is ready only after its aetheric tunnel has developed"@en, "Der Spiegel der Finsternis ist erst bereit, wenn sich sein ätherischer Tunnel gebildet hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only after the aetheric tunnel has developed is the mirror of darkness ready for use."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicFarSeeing ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorOfDarkness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Before the tunnel has developed the mirror is not ready for use." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAethericWeapon a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical weapon: an aetheric duplicate of a blade or dart kept in its material base until hurled"@en, "Die magische Waffe: ein ätherisches Doppel einer Klinge oder eines Pfeils, in seiner stofflichen Grundlage gehalten, bis es geschleudert wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical weapons are made by building an aetheric duplicate of an existing device, a wand, sword, dagger, pointed bone or dart, kept inside the material base until projected by a strong focusing of the will. Personal blood sacrifice may be made to it, or it may be the focus of an orgiastic rite and anointed with sexual fluids; with or without these, intense and prolonged concentration is what imbues such devices with power. Weapons and amulets of great power are sometimes given personal names by which they are controlled."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStrikingAtADistance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hurling the aetheric weapon at a target, through the mirror or at close range"@en, "Die ätherische Waffe auf ein Ziel schleudern, durch den Spiegel oder aus der Nähe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Skilled sorcerers are said to reach through the mirror of darkness to the target or victim and hurl the magical weapon down after it; otherwise close proximity or even contact with the target is required."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWeaponYieldsStriking a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The aetheric weapon is said to be projected at a target by a strong focusing of will"@en, "Die ätherische Waffe soll durch starke Willenssammlung auf ein Ziel geworfen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aetheric form is kept in its base until projected forth by a strong focusing of the will."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAethericWeapon ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicStrikingAtADistance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMirrorBeforeDistance a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Without the mirror of darkness the weapon needs close proximity or contact"@en, "Ohne den Spiegel der Finsternis braucht die Waffe große Nähe oder Berührung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Skilled sorcerers reach through the mirror of darkness to the target and hurl the weapon after it; close proximity or even contact with the target is otherwise required."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicWeaponYieldsStriking ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorOfDarkness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the mirror of darkness the weapon reaches its target only at close range or by contact." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIndependentDevicesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Powerful amulets and weapons have acted independently of incompetent holders"@en, "Mächtige Amulette und Waffen haben unabhängig von unfähigen Besitzern gehandelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Amulets and weapons of great power have sometimes acted quite independently of the incompetents into whose hands they occasionally fall."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAethericWeapon ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Sorcery, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDouble a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The double: an aetheric body without fixed shape, held by the life force in the body's image"@en, "Das Doppel: ein ätherischer Leib ohne feste Gestalt, von der Lebenskraft im Bild des Körpers gehalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The double is said to be described in all magical traditions, from shamanism through the Egyptian Ka and the Ki of the occult martial arts to the soul, the ghost and the modern astral body, and to be most often seen or experienced when the physical body passes close to death. It has no fixed shape, though the life force tends to hold it in the body's image, and even exteriorised it is not necessarily visible; its effects on ordinary reality vary with the ability of the life force to make an effect coalesce, so that it can pass through solid matter and at other times be tangible enough to affect material events. Real dreams, as against fantasy dreams, are called the key to it, and dreaming the most challenging and complete method of freeing it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, pp. 63-64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicProjectingTheForce a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Projecting the double's force beyond the body by visualisation and a sharp yell"@en, "Die Kraft des Doppels durch Visualisierung und einen scharfen Schrei über den Körper hinaus werfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the occult martial arts the double is projected just beyond the striking surfaces of the body by visualisation and a sharp yell that accompany the blow; it may be projected beyond the body to sense the movement of enemies behind, or to the body's surfaces to ward off blows. In most psychic healing the same force is projected, commonly through the hands. Visualisation alone is called the weakest technique, but it can serve as a model on which to build the peculiar bodily sensation that comes from movements in the aether; one persists with imagining until a sensation develops, and sharp, high yells and exhalations help project the force."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: movement in the aether is felt as heat, as a sort of itching, or as an ache."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, pp. 63-64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOccultMartialArts a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The martial uses of the projected force: the delayed death touch, sensing enemies behind, warding off blows"@en, "Die kriegerischen Anwendungen der geworfenen Kraft: die verzögerte Todesberührung, das Spüren von Feinden im Rücken, das Abwehren von Schlägen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three uses of the double's force named at one place: a portion of aetheric force left inside an opponent to cause the delayed death touch; the force projected beyond the body to inform one of the movement of enemies behind; and the force projected to the body's surfaces to ward off blows. Liber AOM adds that force drawn to the hands can deal fatal blows to an enemy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicProjectionYieldsMartialArts a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Projecting the double's force is said to give the delayed death touch and the warding of blows"@en, "Das Werfen der Kraft des Doppels soll die verzögerte Todesberührung und die Abwehr von Schlägen geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Projected with the blow, behind the back or to the surfaces of the body, the force serves each of the three martial uses."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicProjectingTheForce ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicOccultMartialArts ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicHealing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Healing by the force projected through the hands"@en, "Heilen durch die durch die Hände geworfene Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "In most forms of psychic healing the force of the double is said to be projected, commonly through the hands; Liber AOM repeats that force drawn to the hands may be projected just beyond them for healing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicProjectionYieldsHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The force projected through the hands is said to heal"@en, "Die durch die Hände geworfene Kraft soll heilen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The same force as in the martial arts is projected through the hands in psychic healing."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicProjectingTheForce ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicHealing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBeastForm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The double in beast form: strange prowess within the body, and the animal's mode of travel when projected"@en, "Das Doppel in Tiergestalt: seltsame Kraft im Körper, und die Fortbewegung des Tieres, wenn es geworfen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The double may be made to take other forms, most commonly an animal's. Kept within the body, the aetheric beast form may manifest as strange physical prowess and the ability to cow wild animals and confuse and frighten humans; projected beyond it, it can serve as a vehicle for consciousness to experience the animal's mode of travel and abilities. Skilled magicians may attempt composite forms like gryphons and basilisks as magical vehicles."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAtavismCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Theriomorphic forms are atavistic, possess one with the animal's behaviour, and terrify"@en, "Tiergestaltige Formen sind atavistisch, besetzen einen mit dem Verhalten des Tieres und erschrecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beast-like manifestations of the double are often atavistic and cause a form of possession by the behaviour patterns of the animal, whether from dormant memory or from aetheric memories; whatever their source, these atavisms are said to create terrifying effects."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicBeastForm ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNarcosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Narcosis: solanaceous ointments and hallucinogens to reach the double"@en, "Narkose: Salben aus Nachtschattengewächsen und Halluzinogene, um das Doppel zu erreichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since time immemorial magicians are said to have smeared themselves with pastes of the solanaceous alkaloids, thornapple, nightshade and henbane, and consumed other hallucinogens and trance-inducing drugs to gain access to the double."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNarcosisCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Narcosis is the least controllable and most dangerous way to the double"@en, "Die Narkose ist der am wenigsten beherrschbare und gefährlichste Weg zum Doppel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Of all the techniques of gaining access to the double, narcosis is called the least controllable and the most dangerous."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicNarcosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDreamHands a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing the hands of one's double in a real dream, concentrated on every night before sleep"@en, "Die Hände des eigenen Doppels in einem wirklichen Traum sehen, jede Nacht vor dem Schlaf darauf gesammelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first step in creating the double is to establish it in a real dream. The hands are the most easily visible part of the body and dream consciousness is linked to sight, so the magician strives to see the hands of his double in dream, concentrating intently on that desire before sleep every night for as long as it takes. Meanwhile the hands may appear in ordinary idle dreams, which may grow complex and bizarre; this is not the desired result."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: success is abrupt and discontinuous; the command to see one's hands is suddenly remembered as one realises one is dreaming, the hands are there in full clear view, and the shock is like being rudely woken from daydreaming or bursting through a membrane. Condition of failure: hands in ordinary, idle dreams are named as not the result."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShockAwakeningCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The shock of success may wake the dreamer; the experience is to be repeated"@en, "Der Schock des Gelingens kann den Träumer wecken; die Erfahrung ist zu wiederholen"@de ;
    skos:definition "To prevent the shock of success causing awakening, the experience should be repeated several times."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicDreamHands ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTravel a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Travelling in the double to a place known by day, returning to the hands when the vision fades"@en, "Im Doppel an einen bei Tag bekannten Ort reisen, zu den Händen zurückkehrend, wenn die Schau verblasst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician resolves to see a particular place that he also visits in waking hours. Summoning his hands in dream, he looks at them, moves them aside, and tries to find the place he has willed to go to; if the vision fades he goes back to his hands and tries again. He strives to get every detail of the place right and to be there at the same time of day as the dream."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: eventually the double is found actually to be at the desired location. The place is one visited in waking life and the time of day is fixed, so this is the one sign in Liber Null that names checkable details; the work does not say that they are to be checked, nor against what."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicProjectingTheDouble a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The double present at a chosen real place, and no limit beyond"@en, "Das Doppel an einem gewählten wirklichen Ort anwesend, und darüber hinaus keine Grenze"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician eventually finds that his double actually is at the desired location; when this much has been attained, there is said to be no limit to what he may eventually achieve."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamTravelYieldsProjection a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Dream travel from the hands is said to bring the double to the willed place"@en, "Die Traumreise von den Händen aus soll das Doppel an den gewollten Ort bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Returning to the hands whenever the vision fades and striving for every detail, the magician eventually finds the double actually at the desired location."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTravel ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicProjectingTheDouble ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHandsBeforeTravel a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The double is established in a real dream by the hands before it is sent to a place"@en, "Das Doppel wird durch die Hände in einem wirklichen Traum gefestigt, bevor es an einen Ort geschickt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first step in creating the double is to establish it in a real dream through the hands; then the magician resolves to see a particular place."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicDreamTravel ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicDreamHands ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, pp. 64-65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLifelongNightsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "One must be prepared to devote every night of the rest of one's life to these powers"@en, "Man muss bereit sein, jede Nacht des restlichen Lebens diesen Kräften zu widmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Though there is no limit to what may eventually be achieved, one must be prepared to devote every night of the rest of one's life to developing these powers."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicProjectingTheDouble ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransmogrification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Transmogrification: the metamorphosis to black magical consciousness, and Anon"@en, "Transmogrifikation: die Metamorphose zum schwarzmagischen Bewusstsein, und Anon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaos, the life force of the universe, is not human-hearted, so the wizard who taps it cannot be either: he performs monstrous and arbitrary acts to loosen the hold of human limitations on himself. Among the titles of Kia is Anon, which freely transmogrifies its arbitrary personality and refuses any identity defined by its environment, having choice of duality; everything that exists for it is a form of desire, and if this world were believed to be either heaven or hell one would feel free to do anything, only the fear that it is neither imprisoning us. The idea of mind or ego as a fixed possession of Self is illusory; the meaning one experiences is proportional to Kia's manifestation in one's circumstances, and Kia is felt as meaningfulness, power, genius and ecstasy in action, outside of which nothing is true. In the arena of Anon compete selves, souls, familiar spirits, demons and obsessions, each game short before the pieces are hurled through death into new configurations, and only the style and spirit of Anon's play survive unless the aetheric body has achieved great integration."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success, as the work gives it for the manifestation of Kia: meaningfulness, power, genius and ecstasy in action."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, pp. 66-67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAbandoningFixedPatterns a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Abandoning all fixed patterns of residence, employment, relationship and taste"@en, "Alle festen Muster von Wohnsitz, Erwerb, Beziehung und Geschmack aufgeben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical life demands the abandonment of comfort, conventionality, security and safety for competition, combat, extremes and adversity; an air of desperation and a life lived by one's wits close to the edge are required. Therefore the wizard abandons all fixed patterns of residence, employment, relationship and taste."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical consciousness, in which belief, the subtle forces and divination take hold"@en, "Magisches Bewusstsein, in dem Glaube, die feinen Kräfte und die Divination greifen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only in extremes is the spirit said to discover itself, and only a fluid environment can serve as a vessel for magical consciousness, conform to beliefs about it and be subject to the subtle magical forces; only in mutable circumstances can divination come into its own. The transmutation to magical consciousness is completed in the work of the adept, who becomes one who lives Chaos in perpetuity, and transubstantiation is called the achievement of constant magical consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAbandoningPatternsYieldsMagicalConsciousness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A fluid life is said to be the vessel of magical consciousness"@en, "Ein flüssiges Leben soll das Gefäß des magischen Bewusstseins sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Competition, combat, extremes and adversity are said to produce higher resolutions and personal evolution, and a fluid environment to be the vessel of magical consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAbandoningFixedPatterns ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFluidEnvironmentBeforeMagicalConsciousness a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A fluid environment is required as the vessel for magical consciousness"@en, "Eine flüssige Umgebung ist als Gefäß des magischen Bewusstseins erforderlich"@de ;
    skos:definition "A fluid environment is called required as a vessel for magical consciousness; only it can conform to beliefs and be subject to the subtle forces."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work names what comes of the stagnant life."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalConsciousness ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicAbandoningFixedPatterns ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "In a stagnant environment the body-mind creates its own adversity, disease and fantasy." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSigilOfChaosAtDeath a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Visualising the Sigil of Chaos at death to find one's way back to one's occult learning"@en, "Bei Todeseintritt die Sigille des Chaos visualisieren, um zum eigenen okkulten Wissen zurückzufinden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The acts of the black magician bind him to earth forever; if he fears he will not find his way back to his previous occult learning, he may strongly visualise the Sigil of Chaos of figure 7 at his death. The sigil is the sole symbol of the order, a device of recognition and a mirror of darkness for communication between its adepts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReturnToOccultLearning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Finding one's way back to one's previous occult learning in the next life"@en, "In einem nächsten Leben zum früheren okkulten Wissen zurückfinden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the visualised Sigil of Chaos is for: the black magician, bound to earth, finding his way back after death to the occult learning of his previous incarnation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSigilOfChaosYieldsReturn a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Sigil of Chaos visualised at death is offered as the way back to one's occult learning"@en, "Die beim Tod visualisierte Sigille des Chaos wird als Weg zurück zum okkulten Wissen angeboten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician fearful of losing his way may strongly visualise the sigil at death."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSigilOfChaosAtDeath ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicReturnToOccultLearning ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBoundToEarthCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The acts of the black magician bind him to earth forever"@en, "Die Taten des schwarzen Magiers binden ihn für immer an die Erde"@de ;
    skos:definition "The acts of the black magician are said to bind him to earth forever."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicTransmogrification ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Transmogrification, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicQuadrigaSexualis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Quadriga Sexualis: four somatic trances of the Rites of Thanateros and their magical uses"@en, "Die Quadriga Sexualis: vier somatische Trancen der Riten des Thanateros und ihr magischer Gebrauch"@de ;
    skos:definition "A series of somatic trances involving the death posture and the Rites of Thanateros, aligning the magical will with forms of gnostic exaltation. Figure 8 sets out the four and their uses: the death posture for transcendence, normal congress for inspiration, autoerotic congress for reification, and reverse congress, anal or oral, for exhaustion. The pinnacle of excitation and the cave of absolute quiescence are said to be the same place, magically and physiologically, where the hawk-vulture of the Self hangs free of desire, ready to hurl itself into any experience."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 68-69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTranscendedDesire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kia in transcended desire, from which it can hurl itself into any magic"@en, "Kia im überschrittenen Begehren, aus dem es sich in jede Magie stürzen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "Left hovering in an immensity, Kia in its condition of transcended desire can hurl itself into any form of magic: inspiration or atavisms can be dredged up from recondite corners of awareness by sigils, and will and perception extended into new dimensions. The death posture is called chief among the rites of Thanateros because it can inspire, reify and exhaust desire as well as transcend it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 69-70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathPostureYieldsTranscendedDesire a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The death posture is said to leave Kia in transcended desire, ready for any magic"@en, "Die Todeshaltung soll Kia im überschrittenen Begehren lassen, bereit für jede Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The eyes closed and the void entered as completely as possible, Kia is left hovering in an immensity, from which it can hurl itself into any form of magic."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTranscendedDesire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 68-70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStillnessControlsUniverse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "At the moment of most profound stillness the magician controls his universe"@en, "Im Augenblick tiefster Stille beherrscht der Magier sein Universum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The death posture includes all trances meant to bring the mind to complete stillness, and at the moment of the most profound and utter stillness the magician is said to control his universe."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathPostureYieldsControl a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Complete stillness in the death posture is said to give control of one's universe"@en, "Vollkommene Stille in der Todeshaltung soll die Herrschaft über das eigene Universum geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "At the moment of most profound stillness, reached by the trances of the death posture, the magician controls his universe."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicStillnessControlsUniverse ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCrucifixionPosture a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The standing crucifixion: on tiptoe, arms locked behind, the body strained until it collapses into the void, then laughter"@en, "Die stehende Kreuzigung: auf Zehenspitzen, die Arme hinten verschränkt, der Körper gespannt, bis er in die Leere fällt, dann Lachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the mirror death posture proves insufficient to reach gnosis, the magician stands on tiptoe with eyes closed, arms locked behind, neck stretched and back arched, the whole body straining to the limit, the breath deep and spasmodic; oblivious of all but the strain he may attain the void as this too is suddenly removed and he falls exhausted to the floor. Then he evokes the sensation of laughter, reflecting on the meaninglessness of anything as he becomes conscious of it, and laughs aimlessly at everything."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: he may be granted the grace of being swept up into the divine madness of ecstatic laughter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, pp. 69-70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCrucifixionYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The strained posture is said to reach the void when the strain is suddenly removed"@en, "Die gespannte Haltung soll die Leere erreichen, wenn die Spannung plötzlich wegfällt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Oblivious to everything but the strain, the magician may attain the void as the strain is suddenly removed and he falls exhausted."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCrucifixionPosture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisOnePointedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMirrorBeforeCrucifixion a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The crucifixion posture is the recourse when the mirror death posture is insufficient"@en, "Die Kreuzigungshaltung ist der Ausweg, wenn die Spiegel-Todeshaltung nicht genügt"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the foregoing, the mirror death posture, proves insufficient to achieve gnosis, the magician turns to the standing crucifixion."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicCrucifixionPosture ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDivineLaughter a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ecstatic laughter of divine madness, in which conceptualisation and deconceptualisation coincide"@en, "Das ekstatische Lachen des göttlichen Wahnsinns, in dem Begriffsbildung und Begriffsauflösung zusammenfallen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ecstatic laughter of divine madness sweeps every perception into a vortex of surprise at its very existence: everything is suddenly not as it was and yet more exactly as it was, conceptualisation and deconceptualisation occur together, and language turns paradoxical. Such usually accidental paroxysms are said to be cultivable by forms of the death posture and by the wilful evocation of laughter on encountering all things."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 84; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLaughterYieldsDivineLaughter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Wilful laughter at all things is said to cultivate the paroxysm of divine laughter"@en, "Gewolltes Lachen über alle Dinge soll den Anfall göttlichen Lachens heranbilden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The usually accidental paroxysms of divine laughter may be cultivated by wilful evocation of laughter on encountering all things, as after the crucifixion posture, where the magician laughs aimlessly at everything."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLaughterMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivineLaughter ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 84; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathPostureYieldsDivineLaughter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Forms of the death posture are said to cultivate divine laughter"@en, "Formen der Todeshaltung sollen das göttliche Lachen heranbilden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The paroxysms of ecstatic laughter may be cultivated by forms of the death posture."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDeathPosture ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivineLaughter ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 84; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNormalCongress a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Normal congress: priest and priestess in silence, the subject of inspiration abandoned to the subconscious at climax"@en, "Normale Vereinigung: Priester und Priesterin in Stille, der Gegenstand der Eingebung im Höhepunkt dem Unterbewussten überlassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The lunar current of the priestess is observed for a day of strong libido, and the priest strengthens his libido by conservation. In the place of working each reaches silence of mind by a favoured method, for instance kneeling before each other in the dragon position and performing the preliminaries of the death posture. They are then conjoined in a stimulatory embrace, meditating on the subject for inspiration or its sigil as the mountain of excitation is climbed; at the final step the desire is abandoned to the subconscious, and after the cataclysm they remain vacuous but alert to let the inspiration manifest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAmplifiedInspiration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Amplified inspiration for almost anything"@en, "Verstärkte Eingebung für fast alles"@de ;
    skos:definition "Normal congress, the genital embrace of persons of opposite sexes, should in any manifestation inspire the participants with something, if only mutual attachment; employed magically it is said to provide amplified inspiration for almost anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNormalCongressYieldsInspiration a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Normal congress employed magically is said to provide amplified inspiration"@en, "Magisch gebrauchte normale Vereinigung soll verstärkte Eingebung geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The subject abandoned to the subconscious at climax, the celebrants remain vacuous and alert and the inspiration manifests."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicNormalCongress ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAmplifiedInspiration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConservationBeforeCongress a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The priest conserves, and the initiate keeps chastity, before the sexual working"@en, "Der Priester spart auf, und der Initiat hält Keuschheit, vor der sexuellen Arbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the priest the libido is strengthened by conservation before normal congress; in the Alphabet of Desire the magician first observes chastity for a period and then takes every measure to bring himself to the highest point of excitation."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is entered at the practice that names abstention for the regaining of sexuality, the nearest node; the work names no consequence of omitting the conservation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicNormalCongress ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicRegainingSexuality ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualTranscendence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ordinary lust transcended into new peaks of excitation and something else altogether"@en, "Gewöhnliche Lust überstiegen in neue Gipfel der Erregung und in etwas ganz anderes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sexuality is said most often to bring a fleeting glimpse of ecstasy; after a period of chastity and every measure to reach the highest excitation, ordinary lust is transcended, having served its purpose, and consciousness soars to new peaks of excitation and may pass into something else altogether."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: consciousness soaring and passing into something else altogether."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChastityYieldsSexualTranscendence a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Chastity followed by the highest excitation is said to transcend ordinary lust"@en, "Keuschheit, gefolgt von höchster Erregung, soll die gewöhnliche Lust übersteigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "After a period of chastity the magician brings himself to the highest point of excitation, and ordinary lust is transcended."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSexualGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSexualTranscendence ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAutoeroticReification a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Autoerotic congress: the mind blank, the will and perception thrown onto the sigil in the orgasm"@en, "Autoerotische Vereinigung: der Geist leer, Wille und Wahrnehmung im Orgasmus auf die Sigille geworfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mind is kept blank while the sexuality is ignited and brought to a pitch by touch alone, the body supine, the eyes closed and the other senses annulled as far as possible; as the initiate climbs the mountain of excitation the mind rejects all images and fantasies. As the body goes into orgasm, and in the seconds after, the whole force of will and perception is focused on the desire, or more conveniently its sigil."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reification: the making real of a desire"@en, "Verdinglichung: das Wirklichwerden eines Begehrens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The reification, or making real, of a desire is said to be possible through the autoerotic mode: in the brief instant when the initiate is no more, the alignment is made, the obsession formed, the demon born or the sigil charged, and his will sent forth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAutoeroticYieldsReification a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Autoerotic congress is said to make a desire real in the instant of orgasm"@en, "Die autoerotische Vereinigung soll ein Begehren im Augenblick des Orgasmus wirklich machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the instant when he is no more the sigil is charged and the will sent forth."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAutoeroticReification ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicReification ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicExhaustionOfDesire a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Exhaustion of desire: the conscious desire concentrated on through every phase of congress until the mind sickens of it, then banished"@en, "Erschöpfung des Begehrens: das bewusste Begehren durch alle Phasen der Vereinigung festgehalten, bis der Geist seiner überdrüssig wird, dann verbannt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth of the quadriga, reverse congress, works on the principle that wished-for events so often occur after they have been consciously forgotten, the life force then acting through aetheric tensions we created and are no longer aware of. The chosen desire, in its conscious form rather than as a sigil, is concentrated on through all phases of arousal, discharge and aftermath for as long as it takes until the mind begins to react against it or get sick of it; as soon as the reaction appears, the whole is banished from the mind by a forcible turning of attention to other matters."@en ;
    skos:note "The onset of the mind's reaction is the cue to banish, not a sign of success; it was not counted as one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExhaustionYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "With conscious desire and reaction annulled, the desire is said to manifest at some future time"@en, "Sind bewusstes Begehren und Gegenwirkung aufgehoben, soll das Begehren zu einer künftigen Zeit eintreten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conscious desire and reaction annulled, the desire will manifest at some time in the future, the life force acting through the aetheric tensions first created."@en ;
    skos:note "No time is set: the desire is to manifest at some time in the future, and the work gives no span after which it counts as not having done so."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicExhaustionOfDesire ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Ecstasy, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRandomBelief a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Random belief: living each stage of the belief cycle for a week, a month or a year, chosen by the die"@en, "Zufälliger Glaube: jede Stufe des Glaubenszyklus eine Woche, einen Monat oder ein Jahr leben, durch den Würfel gewählt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Six stages of the belief cycle of the self are given, to be tried each or any for a week, a month or a year. They stand in a circle, the first following on from the sixth, and atheism and chaoism are given in early and degenerate phases to make the stages of change clear and to permit the use of the sacred cube, the die."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefCycle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cycle of six beliefs: paganism, monotheism, atheism, nihilism, chaoism, superstition"@en, "Der Kreis der sechs Glauben: Heidentum, Monotheismus, Atheismus, Nihilismus, Chaoismus, Aberglaube"@de ;
    skos:definition "The six dice options, each written in its own voice: paganism, in which gods born of Chaos show themselves in all things and give them essences, good being what pleases them; monotheism, one creator who rewards and punishes, with angels, demons and an established hierarchy; atheism, in which God and soul are unneeded hypotheses and everything has a cause; nihilism, late atheism, in which material causality explains everything and nothing has meaning or free will; chaoism, in which the life force, aether and matter in us are those of the universe and to Chaos nothing is true and everything permitted, though it has limited itself to duality; and superstition, low chaoism, with its principles of contagion and similarity, omens in everything, and the next incarnation as the creature one's life most resembles."@en ;
    skos:note "The option on chaoism refers the reader to The Book of Chaos in its entirety for a further elucidation; that work is not otherwise identified in the text and was not looked for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, pp. 72-75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSparedIncarnation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sparing oneself an unnecessary incarnation or two"@en, "Sich eine oder zwei unnötige Verkörperungen ersparen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exercise of random belief may, the work says, save one an unnecessary incarnation or two."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAeonicMechanism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing the aeonic mechanism that creates the psychic millennia of history"@en, "Den äonischen Mechanismus sehen, der die seelischen Jahrtausende der Geschichte hervorbringt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Random belief may also help to make clear the aeonic mechanism that creates the various psychic millennia of past and future history."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRandomBeliefYieldsSparedIncarnation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Living through the belief cycle is said to save an unnecessary incarnation or two"@en, "Das Durchleben des Glaubenszyklus soll eine oder zwei unnötige Verkörperungen ersparen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Trying each stage for a week, a month or a year may save an unnecessary incarnation or two."@en ;
    skos:note "The effect is placed beyond death; no observation in this life could show that it failed."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRandomBelief ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSparedIncarnation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRandomBeliefYieldsAeonicMechanism a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Living through the belief cycle is said to make the aeonic mechanism clear"@en, "Das Durchleben des Glaubenszyklus soll den äonischen Mechanismus deutlich machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exercise may help to make clear the mechanism behind the psychic millennia."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRandomBelief ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAeonicMechanism ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, Random Belief, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAlphabetOfDesire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Alphabet of Desire: the root emotions as complementary dualisms, each in three modes, with glyphs"@en, "Das Alphabet des Begehrens: die Grundgefühle als einander ergänzende Dualismen, jedes in drei Weisen, mit Glyphen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Except for laughter, which is its own opposite, emotion follows a dual pattern, and the alphabet arranges all basic root emotions as complementary dualisms in a form suggestive of the classical gods or the Ruach of Kabbalah, since pagan pantheons are giant reflections of human qualities. Each principle manifests in three forms named by the alchemical principles: the Mercurial, exalting, cathartic, ecstatic and gnostic; the Sulphurous, the ordinary functional drive; and the Earthy or Salt, heavy and sluggish, when the emotion is baulked or tainted with its opposite and turns in on itself. Table 2 sets the greater duality, attraction against repulsion: sex and death, love and hate, desire and fear, pleasure and pain, elation and depression; the root of every emotion is said always to be its opposite. The twenty-one principles each have a pictorial glyph usable in spells and sigils and a one-word mnemonic, may be equated with the trumps of the Tarot, Kia with the Fool, and a supplementary alphabet of four covers the somatic emotions, corresponding to the four elements. The alphabet may be called the Arena of Anon, which Kia, once free from identification, may wander as it wills."@en ;
    skos:note "The pairs named in the text are sex and death; lust and destructiveness; atrophy and frustration; fear and desire; the reactions to threatening and inviting stimuli; aversion and greed; rage and rapture or passion; love and aggression; loathing and attachment; laughter with deconceptualisation, conceptualisation and union; and in the supplementary alphabet pain and pleasure, depression and elation. In the full-text layer read the glyph lines of pp. 79-86 are lost and several pair headings survive only in part; the pairs are given here as the paragraphs name them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, pp. 76-79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, pp. 84-87; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTransmutingEmotion a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Transmuting a negative or baulked emotion by fixing an alternative desire or its sigil in its climate"@en, "Ein negatives oder gestautes Gefühl umwandeln, indem man in seinem Klima ein anderes Begehren oder dessen Sigille festhält"@de ;
    skos:definition "Anticipating the earthy dysfunctional modes, the magician may transmute their energies and obtain satisfaction in other forms: an alternative desire, or its sigil, is made the focus of concentration in the climate of a negative emotion. Where satisfaction of an emotion is baulked and cannot rise to ecstasy, whatever one was emotional about is forgotten and another desire, magical or mundane, even the desire for laughter, is substituted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 87; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransmutingEmotionYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A desire concentrated on in the climate of a negative emotion is said soon to be realised"@en, "Ein Begehren, im Klima eines negativen Gefühls festgehalten, soll bald verwirklicht werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The alternative desire or its sigil made the focus in a negative emotion will soon be realised, the work says twice, and the trapped energy is turned to other purposes."@en ;
    skos:note "Soon is the only span given; it is not stated in days or weeks."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTransmutingEmotion ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 87; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIndulgenceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Indulging lust or destructiveness for entertainment surely evokes frustration and atrophy"@en, "Lust oder Zerstörungswut zur Unterhaltung auszuleben ruft sicher Frustration und Verkümmerung hervor"@de ;
    skos:definition "As frustration is baulked lust, so the boredom, laziness, depression and self-disgust of atrophy are a failure to destroy or separate oneself from undesirable events, and the attempt to capitalise on one's lust or destructiveness by indulgence for entertainment is called a sure evocation of frustration and atrophy."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicAlphabetOfDesire ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 80; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicJoyGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The gnosis of joy, which can tip the scales into mystic perception"@en, "Die Gnosis der Freude, die die Waage zur mystischen Wahrnehmung hin kippen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "The gnosis of joy is called more difficult to attain than that of fear, but a flight of geese against a sunset, contemplation of religious imagery or intense nostalgia have for some been enough to tip the scales of mystic perception."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, pp. 80-81; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRageAndRapture a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Raving blind rage as a magical state, and all-consuming love as a way into the mystic void"@en, "Rasende blinde Wut als magischer Zustand, und alles verzehrende Liebe als Weg in die mystische Leere"@de ;
    skos:definition "Giving vent to mild anger is called cathartic, clearing the head of tension and relaxing the body; raving blind rage is called a magical state of mind, useful for casting one's will upon the universe and for skilled practitioners a gateway to trance. Entrancement is also a feature of rapture: bhakti yoga, the way of love of a god, has western parallels, and the force of all-consuming love may carry one into the power of the mystic void."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 82; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSuppressingAngerCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Suppressing anger damages the emotional constitution and loses the rapture of love"@en, "Die Unterdrückung des Zorns schädigt die Gefühlsverfassung und verliert die Verzückung der Liebe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seeking to avoid violence we have made a virtue of suppressing anger, which is said to damage the emotional constitution; denying oneself anger, one loses all the rapture of love."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicRageAndRapture ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 81; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeconceptualization a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Deconceptualisation: laughter at shattered expectation, by which all losses can be shrugged off"@en, "Begriffsauflösung: Lachen über zerschlagene Erwartung, mit dem sich alle Verluste abschütteln lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shattering of expectations is the device of all the best humour, the free energy of destroyed beliefs manifesting as laughter. The function is called protective: if we did not laugh at broken expectation we would go mad eventually. By the amoral cultivation of laughter the magician can shrug off all losses and, if he wishes, avoid entering averse states altogether."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets laughter against science, which cannot explain it away, and religion, whose piety it deflates; no text of either is cited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 83; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLaughterYieldsDeconceptualization a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The amoral cultivation of laughter is said to let the magician shrug off all losses"@en, "Die amoralische Pflege des Lachens soll den Magier alle Verluste abschütteln lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By the amoral cultivation of laughter the magician can shrug off all losses and avoid averse states altogether."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLaughterMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDeconceptualization ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 83; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFreedomOfDesire a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A measure of freedom of desire, won by knowing the cycle of desire"@en, "Ein Maß an Freiheit des Begehrens, gewonnen durch die Kenntnis des Kreislaufs des Begehrens"@de ;
    skos:definition "We are ever confined by the dualities of pleasure and pain, happiness and misery, and war, fear, pain and death are the inevitable accompaniment of love, desire, pleasure and sex; only laughter comes free. There is no escape from the cycle of desire, but armed with this knowledge a measure of freedom of desire is said to be won, and those who seek satisfaction in what repels as well as what attracts may partake of the dual ecstasy forever."@en ;
    skos:note "Those who seek to avoid suffering by avoiding desire are called poor fools with small desires and small sufferings; a sage is quoted for the saying that desire is the cause of sorrow. The renouncing position is not attributed to a named work, so no dispute was entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 87; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Alphabet of Desire, p. 81; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAeons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five aeons of consciousness: shamanic, pagan, monotheistic, atheistic, and the coming chaoist aeon"@en, "Fünf Äonen des Bewusstseins: schamanisch, heidnisch, monotheistisch, atheistisch und der kommende chaoistische Äon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Humanity is said to have evolved through four states of consciousness, or aeons, with a fifth on the horizon. The first, from the dawn of humanity, was of shamanism and magic, when rulers had a firm grasp of the psychic forces; it survives in underground witchcraft and sorcery and in aboriginal cultures. The pagan aeon, about six thousand years ago, came with agriculture and cities, as knowledge of the psychic forces grew confused and gods and superstition filled the gaps. The monotheistic aeon, two and a half to three thousand years ago, failed once in Egypt and came into its own with Judaism, Christianity and Islam, in the East as Buddhism, men worshipping an idealised form of themselves. The atheistic aeon, some hundreds of years old, holds that the universe can be understood and manipulated by observing material things and takes emotional experience as the only ground of meaning. Cultures keep residues of earlier aeons, and transitions come ever faster. The evolution is an upward spiral, and the fifth aeon returns to the consciousness of the first in a higher form, chaoist philosophy becoming again a dominant intellectual and moral force and psychic powers being looked to for solutions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Millenium, pp. 88-89; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFifthAeonProphecy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The prophecy of the fifth aeon: five deaths and five technologies"@en, "Die Weissagung des fünften Äons: fünf Tode und fünf Technologien"@de ;
    skos:definition "Decades or centuries of warfare are foreseen: monotheism collapsing before secular humanism and consumerism, technological atheist super-states seeking a stranglehold on consciousness, possibly a destructive war or an anti-technological jihad, which would only affect the timing of the movement of consciousness and not its course. The Illuminati must be ready to exploit five changes: the death of spirituality, as an emotional technology is refined; the death of superstition before a magical technology, telepathy, telekinesis, mind influence, hypnosis, fascination and charisma being examined and exploited as methods of control; the death of identity, a transmutation technology of lifestyle consultants becoming the new priests and magicians; the death of belief, a psychological technology making truth a matter of who creates it, so that reality becomes magical; and the death of ideology, a political technology preserving government. Liber AOM's chapter on aeonics adds that the fifth aeon may become an Aquarian age or a totalitarian tyranny, a great renaissance or a new dark age, and that adepts may break their meditation to alter the course of history."@en ;
    skos:note "A prophecy with no date. Every outcome the text names, renaissance, tyranny, war or new dark age, is listed in advance as compatible with it, and the delay by catastrophe is said to affect only the timing. As stated it cannot fail."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Millenium, pp. 89-91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aeonics, p. 102; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEmotionalTechnologyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Chemicals, electronics and surgery will enslave; gnosis and the Alphabet of Desire liberate"@en, "Chemie, Elektronik und Chirurgie werden versklaven; Gnosis und das Alphabet des Begehrens befreien"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the coming emotional technology, chemicals, electronics and surgery are said only to tend to enslave, while gnosis, the Alphabet of Desire and other magical methods tend to liberate; magicians may be seen working behind barbed wire and in underground cells."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicFifthAeonProphecy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, The Millenium, p. 90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAeonicWork a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The aeonic work of adepts: building the new aeon's technologies and founding cults, orders and cabals"@en, "Die äonische Arbeit der Adepten: die Technologien des neuen Äons bauen und Kulte, Orden und Kabalen gründen"@de ;
    skos:definition "As agents of the fifth aeon adepts may work to build its technologies, of emotion, belief, magic, transmutation and politics, which will free men from spirituality, prejudice, superstition, identity and ideology; and they may create cults, orders, covens and cabals to spread the secret wisdom and to destroy the satraps of old-aeon spirituality and its thought forms. Existence is called the chariot of Chaos and man its most refined vehicle, and adepts may see to it that society evolves better forms to support the divine incarnation of Kia on earth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aeonics, p. 102; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAlteringHistory a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Altering the course of history toward the fifth aeon"@en, "Den Lauf der Geschichte auf den fünften Äon hin verändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Adepts, as agents of the fifth aeon Illuminati, may occasionally break their meditation to alter the course of history; the erection of operating chaospheres at points about the earth is said to hasten the immanentisation of the eschaton, the change of aeon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aeonics, p. 102; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, p. 101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAeonicWorkYieldsAlteringHistory a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The adepts' aeonic work is offered as the way to shape the fifth aeon"@en, "Die äonische Arbeit der Adepten wird als Weg angeboten, den fünften Äon zu formen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Building the new technologies and founding cults and orders is the work by which adepts may alter the course of history toward the new aeon."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAeonicWork ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAlteringHistory ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aeonics, p. 102; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAdeptWork a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The work of the adept: above all symbolic systems but reality, measured only by the ability to work magic"@en, "Die Arbeit des Adepten: über allen Symbolsystemen außer der Wirklichkeit, gemessen nur an der Fähigkeit, Magie zu wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rituals of the adept's degree are said to be secret, but stated here in the most explicit form language permits, so that some sight of the goal may be had. The adept has risen above all symbolic systems save reality: sigils, gods, demons and the instruments of the sorcerer he reabsorbs or keeps only for teaching, his magical weapons are ceremonial, and his tools are direct prescience and enchantment. Where knowledge and ecstasy measured progress in the lower grades, only the ability to work magic measures the adept's spirituality. Liber AOM is laid out in figure 12 under aetherics, transubstantiation, the chaosphere, aeonics and reincarnation."@en ;
    skos:note "The measure the work sets for the adept, the ability to work magic, is the only measure in the book that is not the practitioner's own state; the book gives no procedure for taking it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, pp. 95-96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLivingChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming one who lives Chaos in perpetuity: a centre of creation and destruction, a complete microcosm, a god"@en, "Einer werden, der das Chaos fortwährend lebt: ein Mittelpunkt von Schöpfung und Zerstörung, ein vollständiger Mikrokosmos, ein Gott"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the work of the adept the transmutation to magical consciousness is completed and he becomes one who lives Chaos in perpetuity; the force may take him anywhere, to project the personality dualities needed to gather people into autonomous satraps of the Illuminati, or to withdraw his manifestation from the plane of duality entirely and cease to exist. Magic, unlike mysticism, plays the light and dark powers off against each other, and the magician aims to become a centre of creation and destruction, a living manifestation of the Chaos force within duality, a complete microcosm, a god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLightAndDarkPowers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The light power and the dark: the expanding and the withdrawing expressions of Chaos"@en, "Die Kraft des Lichts und die der Finsternis: die sich ausdehnende und die sich zurückziehende Gestalt des Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Out of Chaos arise the two prime forces of existence, its solve et coagula. The light power is the expanding, outgoing, dualising expression of Chaos, responsible for birth, creation, incarnation and variety; the dark power is the contracting, returning, transcending one, responsible for death, dissolution, reabsorption, simplicity and return to the source. They lie at the root of all mystic quests and all magical and mundane action; adherents of one always call the other black, which the work calls mere moral philosophy. The positive and negative paths of magic converge into a unity that diverges from the mystic paths."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInitiateBeforeAdept a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Only perfection in the initiate's work gives the empowerment to use the adept's techniques"@en, "Nur die Vollendung in der Arbeit des Initiaten gibt die Ermächtigung, die Techniken des Adepten zu gebrauchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only by perfecting oneself in the work of the initiate can one attain the empowerment required to use the techniques of the adept; anything less leads to failure, disaster and death."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure. The perfection required is not described further, so the clause assigns every failure of an adept technique to an imperfect initiate."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicAdeptWork ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicInitiateSyllabuses ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Anything less than perfection in the initiate's work leads to failure, disaster and death." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, introduction, p. 95; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAethers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The aethers in their densities: the dense aether for gross feats, the subtle for thought and emotion"@en, "Die Äther in ihren Dichten: der dichte Äther für grobe Taten, der feine für Gedanke und Gefühl"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aethers in their various degrees of density are called the means by which magic and miracles are effected; the adept may need these abilities to accomplish his will, or develop them as a test of the mystic abilities. Operations are classified by the density and origin of the forces used. The dense aether, for gross feats, resides in the belly just above the navel and may be extruded in lines of force to any part of the body and to points outside it up to several metres away. The subtle aethers, which normally link the life force to the brain, transfer thoughts and emotions; the simpler a message and the greater its emotional charge, the more easily it is projected, and destructive effects are much more easily created than constructive ones."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, pp. 97-98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicFrogJump a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The frog jump: sitting cross-legged in darkness and trying to jump with full lungs"@en, "Der Froschsprung: im Dunkeln mit gekreuzten Beinen sitzen und mit vollen Lungen zu springen versuchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "To get the force lines to come from the body, the adept sits cross-legged on the ground and tries to jump like a frog from that awkward position, usually in darkness and with the lungs fully inflated; a cushion may protect the tissues of the posterior meanwhile."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated span: within as little as three years' practice one may begin to learn how to exert the aetheric force against the ground. Counted as a sign of success and noted as the only span in Liber Null long enough to be a test window; the work does not say what follows if three years pass without it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, pp. 97-98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLevitation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Levitation: walking on water, on fire, in the air, or at fantastic speeds on the force lines of the dense aether"@en, "Levitation: auf Wasser, auf Feuer, in der Luft oder in unglaublicher Geschwindigkeit gehen auf den Kraftlinien des dichten Äthers"@de ;
    skos:definition "Levitation, which is said to include the ability to walk on water and fire as well as in the air, or at fantastic rates across the earth, is accomplished by supporting the body's weight on aetheric force lines; in firewalking the force is used to repel the heat and flames."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFrogJumpYieldsLevitation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The frog jump is the exercise by which the force lines of levitation are drawn from the body"@en, "Der Froschsprung ist die Übung, mit der die Kraftlinien der Levitation aus dem Körper gezogen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The awkward jump with inflated lungs is practised so that the force lines come from the body; after some years one begins to exert the force against the ground."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicFrogJump ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLevitation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, pp. 97-98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSuperhumanFeats a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Superhuman feats of the dense aether: great leaps, clinging to smooth walls, deflecting weapons, the strength of madness"@en, "Übermenschliche Taten des dichten Äthers: große Sprünge, Haften an glatten Wänden, Ablenken von Waffen, die Kraft des Wahnsinns"@de ;
    skos:definition "The dense aetheric force is called the basis of most superhuman feats, each named singly at one place: leaping to great heights, clinging to smooth vertical surfaces, deflecting weapons, and the strength of madness; also the short-range occult martial arts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMovingTheForce a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Moving the force within the body by a warmth or itching at chosen points, and drawing it to the hands"@en, "Die Kraft im Körper bewegen durch eine Wärme oder ein Jucken an gewählten Stellen, und sie in die Hände ziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Movement of the force within the body is called easier to achieve and is most commonly approached by trying to develop a warmth or itching sensation at various points. The adept directs the psychic force to any site of weakness, and if it can be drawn to the hands, projects it just beyond them."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the warmth or itching at the chosen points."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMovingTheForceYieldsFeats a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Moving the dense force is said to underlie superhuman feats"@en, "Das Bewegen der dichten Kraft soll den übermenschlichen Taten zugrunde liegen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The force moved within and beyond the body is the dense aetheric force on which the work rests the superhuman feats."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMovingTheForce ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSuperhumanFeats ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLongevity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freedom from virtually any disease, and longevity, by directing the force to weak sites"@en, "Freiheit von nahezu jeder Krankheit, und langes Leben, indem die Kraft an schwache Stellen gelenkt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The adept should be able to free himself from the attack of virtually any form of disease and ensure longevity by directing the psychic force to any site of weakness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMovingTheForceYieldsLongevity a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Directing the force to sites of weakness is said to free one from disease and ensure longevity"@en, "Das Lenken der Kraft an schwache Stellen soll von Krankheit befreien und langes Leben sichern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Directing the psychic force to any site of weakness frees the adept from virtually any disease."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMovingTheForce ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLongevity ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMovingTheForceYieldsHealing a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Force drawn to the hands is said to be projected beyond them for healing or fatal blows"@en, "In die Hände gezogene Kraft soll über sie hinaus zum Heilen oder zu tödlichen Schlägen geworfen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the force can be drawn to the hands it may be projected just beyond them for healing, or to deal fatal blows to an enemy."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMovingTheForce ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicHealing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPlantWithering a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Withering a small plant by unaided will, then reversing the current to revive it"@en, "Eine kleine Pflanze durch bloßen Willen welken lassen, dann den Strom umkehren und sie wiederbeleben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The adept may begin to strengthen his subtle abilities by selecting a small plant and trying to make it wilt and wither by the force of his unaided will; as it begins to succumb he reverses the current of his will and resuscitates it."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the plant begins to succumb. Of all the signs in Liber Null this one is outside the operator; the work does not ask for a control plant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWitheringAndReviving a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Making a plant wither and revive by unaided will"@en, "Eine Pflanze durch bloßen Willen welken und wieder aufleben lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By unaided will the adept makes a small plant wilt and wither and, reversing the current, resuscitates it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPlantWitheringYieldsWithering a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The plant exercise is said to wither and revive the plant by will"@en, "Die Pflanzenübung soll die Pflanze durch den Willen welken und wiederaufleben lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exercise is offered as the beginning of strengthening the subtle abilities, and its effect is the plant's withering and revival."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPlantWithering ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicWitheringAndReviving ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAnimalTelepathyPractice a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Practising telepathic contact with beasts, dogs above all"@en, "Telepathische Verbindung mit Tieren üben, vor allem mit Hunden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The adept may practise telepathic contact with various beasts, dogs being particularly suitable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAnimalTelepathy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Telepathic contact with animals"@en, "Telepathische Verbindung mit Tieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Telepathic contact with beasts, by the subtle aethers that transfer thought and emotion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAnimalPracticeYieldsTelepathy a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Practice with dogs and other beasts is offered toward telepathic contact"@en, "Übung mit Hunden und anderen Tieren wird zur telepathischen Verbindung angeboten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice is given among the ways the adept strengthens the subtle abilities of thought transference."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAnimalTelepathyPractice ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAnimalTelepathy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCommandingPersons a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "At odd moments picking persons nearby and willing them to stand, sit or move"@en, "In beliebigen Augenblicken Personen in der Nähe wählen und ihnen das Aufstehen, Sitzen oder Gehen anwollen"@de ;
    skos:definition "At odd moments the adept may pick various persons around him and make them stand up, sit down, or move around and do particular things."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMovingPersons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Making other persons stand, sit, move or do particular things by will"@en, "Andere Personen durch den Willen aufstehen, sitzen, gehen oder Bestimmtes tun lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the exercise names: persons nearby made to stand up, sit down, move around or do particular things."@en ;
    skos:note "People stand and sit at odd moments of their own accord; the work gives no way to tell a willed rising from an unwilled one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCommandingYieldsMovingPersons a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Willing people nearby to move is said to make them do so"@en, "Das Anwollen von Menschen in der Nähe soll sie dazu bringen, sich zu bewegen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The adept may make chosen persons stand up, sit down or do particular things."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCommandingPersons ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMovingPersons ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics one wishes to project"@en, "Beständige, halbbewusste, starke Sammlung auf die Eigenschaften, die man ausstrahlen will"@de ;
    skos:definition "The powers of invisibility and charisma are said to arise from a constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics one wishes to project."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibility a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An aura of subjective invisibility: others prevented from noticing one's presence"@en, "Eine Aura subjektiver Unsichtbarkeit: andere werden gehindert, die eigene Anwesenheit zu bemerken"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the classical tests of psychic ability is called the casting of an aura of subjective invisibility: no change in one's optical properties, but those around are somehow prevented from noticing one's presence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCharisma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An aura of charisma or fascination, with uses in founding cults and messianic sects"@en, "Eine Aura des Charismas oder der Faszination, nützlich zur Gründung von Kulten und messianischen Sekten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The complement of invisibility is the projection of an aura of charisma or fascination, which may have application in forming sundry cults and messianic sects."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuraYieldsInvisibility a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Constant concentration on what one wishes to project is said to cast an aura of invisibility"@en, "Beständige Sammlung auf das, was man ausstrahlen will, soll eine Aura der Unsichtbarkeit werfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The power arises from constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics projected."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibility ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuraYieldsCharisma a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The same concentration is said to project an aura of charisma or fascination"@en, "Dieselbe Sammlung soll eine Aura des Charismas oder der Faszination ausstrahlen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charisma, the complementary power, arises from the same concentration."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCharisma ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation: identifying completely in each act, and residing in the void between acts"@en, "Transsubstantiation: sich in jeder Handlung völlig gleichsetzen, und zwischen den Handlungen in der Leere wohnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The penultimate metamorphosis. In all things one knows both that one is this illusion and that one is not this illusion. At the moment of doing one is in it, identifies completely and lives it; in the interstices between all one's doing one resides in the void, vacuous, with no mind."@en ;
    skos:note "The page carries the running head Liber AOM without a folio in the layer read; it lies between pp. 98 and 100."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransubstantiationYieldsMagicalConsciousness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation is said to achieve constant magical consciousness"@en, "Die Transsubstantiation soll beständiges magisches Bewusstsein erreichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The penultimate metamorphosis is called the achievement of constant magical consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFreedomFromConsequences a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions"@en, "Vollständige Freiheit von den Folgen der eigenen Handlungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By full identification in action and vacuity between actions one is said to attain complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransubstantiationYieldsFreedom a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation is said to give complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions"@en, "Die Transsubstantiation soll völlige Freiheit von den Folgen der eigenen Handlungen geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowing that by this one may attain to complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions closes the chapter."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFreedomFromConsequences ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHalfInTheWorldCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nothing is more ineffectual than being always half in the world and half out of it"@en, "Nichts ist wirkungsloser, als immer halb in der Welt und halb aus ihr zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is said to be nothing more ineffectual than to be always only half in the world and half out of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the half-way state is named as the ineffectual one."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

